window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"Every labeled structure on the 74 cadaver photos from the thorax and GI lab, as a “what is this?” question. All labels are covered and the yellow ? marks the one asked; pick its name from five look-alike choices. Each region opens with a short summary of the structures it covers. Each part has short notes, then its core questions (one per structure), then a “More questions” dropdown with the same structures on other pictures. After you answer: where it lies, how to find it, why each other choice is wrong, and more pictures of it.","feat":1,"fold":1,"tier":1,"uw":"Region","type":"cadaver","npic":74},"lectures":[{"id":"CAD-T1","t":"Thoracic wall & bony thorax"},{"id":"CAD-T2","t":"Lungs, pleura & airways"},{"id":"CAD-T3","t":"Mediastinum & great vessels"},{"id":"CAD-T4","t":"Heart & coronary vessels"},{"id":"CAD-A1","t":"Abdominal wall"},{"id":"CAD-A2","t":"Stomach, duodenum & peritoneum"},{"id":"CAD-A3","t":"Liver, gallbladder, pancreas & spleen"},{"id":"CAD-A4","t":"Small & large intestine"},{"id":"CAD-A5","t":"Kidneys & posterior abdominal wall"}],"groups":[{"l":"CAD-T1","u":0,"label":"Internal thoracic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0154,0.2714,0.2528,0.3203]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s025~internal-thoracic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Musculophrenic artery","B. Right pulmonary artery","C. Transversus thoracis muscle","D. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","E. Internal thoracic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal thoracic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Internal thoracic artery.</b> The internal thoracic artery is a branch of the subclavian artery that runs down behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>First part of the subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 1-6; ends as musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and pleura above; transversus thoracis separates it from pleura lower down</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn over the removed anterior chest wall and find the lateral edge of the sternum first; the vertical artery running parallel to it about a finger-width lateral, behind the cartilages, is the internal thoracic artery.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Musculophrenic artery: The musculophrenic artery is the lateral end branch of the internal thoracic artery, leaving it in the 6th intercostal space to run along the costal margin. <b>Comes from internal thoracic artery (its lateral terminal branch, in the 6th intercostal space).</b><br>B. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>C. Transversus thoracis muscle: Transversus thoracis lies on the inner surface of the front chest wall, fanning from the lower sternum and xiphoid to costal cartilages 2–6, behind the internal thoracic vessels.<br>D. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["Know its two terminal branches: the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s025.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 25"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9383,0.4297,0.9924,"",1]],"ar":0.74143}]},{"l":"CAD-T1","u":0,"label":"Transversus thoracis muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_118_1347.jpg","cap":"Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.13","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0094,0.5933,0.1972,0.6393]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s015~transversus-thoracis-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Musculophrenic artery","B. Pectinate muscles","C. Papillary muscle","D. Internal thoracic artery","E. Transversus thoracis muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus thoracis muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Transversus thoracis muscle.</b> Transversus thoracis lies on the inner surface of the front chest wall, fanning from the lower sternum and xiphoid to costal cartilages 2–6, behind the internal thoracic vessels.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Turn the removed anterior chest wall (sternum and cartilages) over and find the internal thoracic artery first; the thin muscle slips covering it and fanning out from the lower sternum are transversus thoracis.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Musculophrenic artery: The musculophrenic artery is the lateral end branch of the internal thoracic artery, leaving it in the 6th intercostal space to run along the costal margin. <b>Comes from internal thoracic artery (its lateral terminal branch, in the 6th intercostal space).</b><br>B. Pectinate muscles: The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<br>C. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>D. Internal thoracic artery: The internal thoracic artery is a branch of the subclavian artery that runs down behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum. <b>Comes from first part of the subclavian artery.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s015.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 15"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1796,0.954,0.5101,0.9954,"",1]],"ar":1.04285}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1067,0.2239,0.2201,0.2678]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q12~superior-lobe-of-right-lung","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Oblique fissure","B. Superior (upper) lobe of left lung","C. Middle lobe of right lung","D. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","E. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung.</b> The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Middle lobe below (horizontal fissure), inferior lobe behind; medially SVC (superior vena cava) and trachea</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Suprapleural membrane at its apex; upper ribs and pectoral muscles in front</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the horizontal fissure first; the lobe above it (and in front of the upper oblique fissure) is the right superior lobe.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Oblique fissure: The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<br>B. Superior (upper) lobe of left lung: In axial sections the left upper lobe is the lung in front of the oblique fissure, forming the apex and front of the left lung, including the lingula beside the heart.<br>C. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>D. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8152,0.5843,0.9936,0.6443,"",0],[0.0064,0.6728,0.1956,0.7329,"",0],[0.8152,0.4958,0.9936,0.5558,"",0],[0.0064,0.5463,0.1956,0.6064,"",0],[0.8152,0.2934,0.9936,0.3534,"",1]],"ar":0.36737}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_141_1401.jpg","cap":"Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.34","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0124,0.5807,0.1488,0.6599]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st30.jpg","cap":"Horizontal fissure (right lung) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 30","ov":[[0.2501,0.9488,0.5547,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q12~horizontal-fissure","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Oblique fissure","B. Middle lobe of right lung","C. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","D. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","E. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Horizontal fissure (right lung).</b> Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Middle lobe below it; meets the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure on the lateral surface of the right lung first; the fissure that branches off it and runs forward horizontally is the horizontal fissure.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Oblique fissure: The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<br>B. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>C. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>E. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8152,0.5843,0.9936,0.6443,"",0],[0.0064,0.6728,0.1956,0.7329,"",0],[0.8152,0.2934,0.9936,0.3534,"",0],[0.0064,0.5463,0.1956,0.6064,"",0],[0.8152,0.4958,0.9936,0.5558,"",1]],"ar":0.36737}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Middle lobe of right lung","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_258.jpg","cap":"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0891,0.4468,0.1994,0.4926]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q12~middle-lobe-of-right-lung","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","B. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","C. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","D. Oblique fissure","E. Middle lobe of right lung"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle lobe of right lung","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Middle lobe of right lung.</b> The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right atrium (right heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the right 4th-6th costal cartilages</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find both fissures of the right lung first; the lobe enclosed between the horizontal fissure and the lower anterior oblique fissure is the middle lobe.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>B. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>C. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>D. Oblique fissure: The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0064,0.6728,0.1956,0.7329,"",0],[0.8152,0.2934,0.9936,0.3534,"",0],[0.8152,0.4958,0.9936,0.5558,"",0],[0.0064,0.5463,0.1956,0.6064,"",0],[0.8152,0.5843,0.9936,0.6443,"",1]],"ar":0.36737}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Oblique fissure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_259.jpg","cap":"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8425,0.3487,0.9933,0.3958]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q13.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 13","ov":[[0.1719,0.946,0.4674,0.9963]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s011.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 11","ov":[[0.2278,0.9532,0.4653,0.9974]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q12~oblique-fissure","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Middle lobe of right lung","B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","C. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","D. Oblique fissure","E. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Oblique fissure","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Oblique fissure.</b> The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>C. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>E. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8152,0.5843,0.9936,0.6443,"",0],[0.0064,0.6728,0.1956,0.7329,"",0],[0.8152,0.2934,0.9936,0.3534,"",0],[0.8152,0.4958,0.9936,0.5558,"",0],[0.0064,0.5463,0.1956,0.6064,"",1]],"ar":0.36737}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0441,0.6955,0.1464,0.7394]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q12~inferior-lobe-of-right-lung","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Middle lobe of right lung","B. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","C. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","D. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","E. Inferior (lower) lobe of left lung"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung.</b> In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right diaphragm dome over the liver; esophagus and IVC (inferior vena cava) medially</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior and middle lobes in front, across the oblique fissure</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure first; the large lobe behind and below it is the inferior lobe (on the right, confirm a horizontal fissure exists in front).<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>B. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>C. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>E. Inferior (lower) lobe of left lung: In axial sections the left lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming the back and base of the left lung beside the descending aorta.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.8152,0.5843,0.9936,0.6443,"",0],[0.8152,0.2934,0.9936,0.3534,"",0],[0.8152,0.4958,0.9936,0.5558,"",0],[0.0064,0.5463,0.1956,0.6064,"",0],[0.0064,0.6728,0.1956,0.7329,"",1]],"ar":0.36737}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_141_1401.jpg","cap":"Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.34","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0124,0.5807,0.1488,0.6599]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","cap":"Horizontal fissure (right lung) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12","ov":[[0.8132,0.4938,0.9956,0.5578]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st30~horizontal-fissure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","B. Oblique fissure","C. Middle lobe of right lung","D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","E. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Horizontal fissure (right lung)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Horizontal fissure (right lung).</b> Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Middle lobe below it; meets the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure on the lateral surface of the right lung first; the fissure that branches off it and runs forward horizontally is the horizontal fissure.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Oblique fissure: The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<br>C. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>E. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st30.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 30"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2521,0.9508,0.5527,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.39952,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q12~horizontal-fissure"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Oblique fissure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_259.jpg","cap":"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8425,0.3487,0.9933,0.3958]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12","ov":[[0.0044,0.5443,0.1976,0.6084]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s011.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 11","ov":[[0.2278,0.9532,0.4653,0.9974]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q13~oblique-fissure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Oblique fissure","B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","C. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","E. Middle lobe of right lung"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Oblique fissure","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Oblique fissure.</b> The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>C. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>E. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q13.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 13"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.948,0.4654,0.9943,"",1]],"ar":0.89143,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q12~oblique-fissure"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":1,"label":"Oblique fissure","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_264_259.jpg","cap":"Views of the chest wall showing the surface projections of the lobes and the fissures of the lungs","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.103","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8425,0.3487,0.9933,0.3958]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q12.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 12","ov":[[0.0044,0.5443,0.1976,0.6084]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q13.jpg","cap":"Oblique fissure on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 13","ov":[[0.1719,0.946,0.4674,0.9963]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s011~oblique-fissure","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Oblique fissure","B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","C. Middle lobe of right lung","D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","E. Horizontal fissure (right lung)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Oblique fissure","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Oblique fissure.</b> The oblique fissure runs in each lung from about T2 behind to the 6th costal cartilage in front, with the inferior lobe behind and below it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>C. Middle lobe of right lung: The right middle lobe is the small wedge between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below, lying in front against the right side of the heart.<br>D. Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: The right upper lobe forms the apex and upper front of the right lung, above the horizontal fissure and in front of the upper oblique fissure.<br>E. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s011.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 11"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.2298,0.9552,0.4633,0.9954,"",1]],"ar":1.41538,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q12~oblique-fissure"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":2,"label":"Lingula (of left lung)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_138_1395.jpg","cap":"Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7605,0.678,0.9369,0.7514]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q14~lingula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","C. Lingula (of left lung)","D. Cardiac notch (left lung)","E. Pulmonary veins"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lingula (of left lung)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lingula (of left lung).</b> The lingula is the tongue-shaped lower front part of the left superior lobe, just below the cardiac notch and over the left side of the heart.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left ventricle (left heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cardiac notch on the anterior border of the left lung first; the tongue of lung tissue curling below and in front of it is the lingula.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>B. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>D. Cardiac notch (left lung): The cardiac notch is the deep scoop in the anterior border of the left lung's upper lobe where the heart pushes in, just above the lingula.<br>E. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q14.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 14"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.933,0.3854,0.9913,"",1]],"ar":0.68}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":3,"label":"Right main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.5771,0.0996,0.6192]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st29.jpg","cap":"Right main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 29","ov":[[0.1795,0.9374,0.4313,0.9941]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s005~right-main-bronchus","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Left main (primary) bronchus","C. Right pulmonary artery","D. Pulmonary veins","E. Left pulmonary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main (primary) bronchus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right main (primary) bronchus.</b> The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>C. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>D. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>E. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3953,0.1763,0.4455,"",0],[0.007,0.4649,0.1763,0.5151,"",0],[0.8334,0.5013,0.993,0.5515,"",0],[0.007,0.5399,0.1763,0.5901,"",0],[0.8334,0.4009,0.993,0.4511,"",0],[0.8334,0.3349,0.993,0.3851,"",0],[0.8334,0.4511,0.993,0.5013,"",0],[0.007,0.2949,0.1763,0.3451,"",1],[0.007,0.3451,0.1763,0.3953,"",1]],"ar":0.48053}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":3,"label":"Left main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.6654,0.0996,0.7078]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","cap":"Left main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15","ov":[[0.0053,0.3849,0.1604,0.452]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s005~left-main-bronchus","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right pulmonary artery","B. Left main (primary) bronchus","C. Pulmonary veins","D. Left pulmonary artery","E. Right main (primary) bronchus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left main (primary) bronchus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left main (primary) bronchus.</b> The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Esophagus and descending aorta behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Aortic arch hooking over it; left pulmonary artery in front, then above</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina first; the long bronchus running left under the aortic arch, below the left pulmonary artery, is the left main bronchus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>C. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>D. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>E. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3953,0.1763,0.4455,"",0],[0.007,0.2949,0.1763,0.3451,"",0],[0.007,0.3451,0.1763,0.3953,"",0],[0.007,0.4649,0.1763,0.5151,"",0],[0.8334,0.5013,0.993,0.5515,"",0],[0.007,0.5399,0.1763,0.5901,"",0],[0.8334,0.4009,0.993,0.4511,"",0],[0.8334,0.3349,0.993,0.3851,"",0],[0.8334,0.4511,0.993,0.5013,"",1]],"ar":0.48053}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":3,"label":"Right main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.5771,0.0996,0.6192]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","cap":"Right main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5","ov":[[0.005,0.2929,0.1783,0.3471],[0.005,0.3431,0.1783,0.3973]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st29~right-main-bronchus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left main (primary) bronchus","B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung","C. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","D. Right main (primary) bronchus","E. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main (primary) bronchus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right main (primary) bronchus.</b> The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>B. Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung: In axial sections the right lower lobe is the lung behind the oblique fissure, forming most of the back and base of the right lung beside the esophagus.<br>C. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>E. Tracheobronchial lymph nodes: The tracheobronchial lymph nodes cluster around the tracheal bifurcation, in the angles between the trachea and each main bronchus and below the carina. <b>Drains into paratracheal nodes, then the bronchomediastinal lymph trunks.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st29.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 29"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.4293,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s005~right-main-bronchus"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":3,"label":"Left main (primary) bronchus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0071,0.6654,0.0996,0.7078]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","cap":"Left main (primary) bronchus on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5","ov":[[0.8314,0.4491,0.995,0.5033]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q15~left-main-bronchus","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Left main (primary) bronchus","B. Right main (primary) bronchus","C. Pulmonary veins","D. Horizontal fissure (right lung)","E. Left pulmonary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left main (primary) bronchus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Left main (primary) bronchus.</b> The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Esophagus and descending aorta behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Aortic arch hooking over it; left pulmonary artery in front, then above</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina first; the long bronchus running left under the aortic arch, below the left pulmonary artery, is the left main bronchus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>C. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>D. Horizontal fissure (right lung): Found only in the right lung, the horizontal fissure runs forward from the oblique fissure along the fourth rib, separating the superior and middle lobes.<br>E. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The left main bronchus is usually inferior to the left pulmonary artery, while the right main bronchus is posterior to the right pulmonary artery."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.8299,0.267,0.9927,0.3301,"",0],[0.8299,0.4222,0.9927,0.4853,"",0],[0.0073,0.5633,0.1584,0.6264,"",0],[0.0073,0.3869,0.1584,0.45,"",1]],"ar":0.37474,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s005~left-main-bronchus"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":4,"label":"Right pulmonary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_145_1414.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary circulation","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.37","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0051,0.1216,0.1487,0.1993]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s005~right-pulmonary-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Right pulmonary artery","C. Left pulmonary artery","D. Left main (primary) bronchus","E. Pulmonary veins"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right pulmonary artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right pulmonary artery.</b> The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk first and follow its right branch; the vessel that disappears behind the ascending aorta and SVC toward the right hilum is the right pulmonary artery.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>C. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>D. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>E. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.2949,0.1763,0.3451,"",0],[0.007,0.3451,0.1763,0.3953,"",0],[0.007,0.4649,0.1763,0.5151,"",0],[0.8334,0.5013,0.993,0.5515,"",0],[0.007,0.5399,0.1763,0.5901,"",0],[0.8334,0.4009,0.993,0.4511,"",0],[0.8334,0.3349,0.993,0.3851,"",0],[0.8334,0.4511,0.993,0.5013,"",0],[0.007,0.3953,0.1763,0.4455,"",1]],"ar":0.48053}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":4,"label":"Left pulmonary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_196_1509.jpg","cap":"(Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0527,0.5925,0.2186,0.6325]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","cap":"Left pulmonary artery on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15","ov":[[0.8279,0.265,0.9947,0.3321]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s005~left-pulmonary-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Pulmonary veins","B. Left pulmonary artery","C. Right main (primary) bronchus","D. Left main (primary) bronchus","E. Right pulmonary artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left pulmonary artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left pulmonary artery.</b> The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Descending aorta behind; it arches over the left main bronchus</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left phrenic nerve in front; aortic arch above, tied to it by the ligamentum arteriosum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum under the aortic arch first; the pulmonary branch it attaches to, heading left over the left bronchus, is the left pulmonary artery.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>C. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>D. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>E. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3953,0.1763,0.4455,"",0],[0.007,0.2949,0.1763,0.3451,"",0],[0.007,0.3451,0.1763,0.3953,"",0],[0.007,0.4649,0.1763,0.5151,"",0],[0.8334,0.5013,0.993,0.5515,"",0],[0.007,0.5399,0.1763,0.5901,"",0],[0.8334,0.4009,0.993,0.4511,"",0],[0.8334,0.4511,0.993,0.5013,"",0],[0.8334,0.3349,0.993,0.3851,"",1]],"ar":0.48053}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":4,"label":"Pulmonary veins","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_202_165.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary vessels","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.45","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1258,0.6287,0.2788,0.6894]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary veins on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15","ov":[[0.8279,0.4202,0.9947,0.4873],[0.0053,0.5613,0.1604,0.6284]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s005~pulmonary-veins","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right main (primary) bronchus","B. Pulmonary veins","C. Left main (primary) bronchus","D. Left pulmonary artery","E. Right pulmonary artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary veins","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pulmonary veins.</b> The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right main (primary) bronchus: The right main bronchus is the short, wide, steep branch going right from the carina, passing under the azygos arch and behind the right pulmonary artery into the hilum.<br>C. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>D. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>E. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3953,0.1763,0.4455,"",0],[0.007,0.2949,0.1763,0.3451,"",0],[0.007,0.3451,0.1763,0.3953,"",0],[0.8334,0.3349,0.993,0.3851,"",0],[0.8334,0.4511,0.993,0.5013,"",0],[0.007,0.4649,0.1763,0.5151,"",1],[0.8334,0.5013,0.993,0.5515,"",1],[0.007,0.5399,0.1763,0.5901,"",1],[0.8334,0.4009,0.993,0.4511,"",1]],"ar":0.48053}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":4,"label":"Left pulmonary artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_196_1509.jpg","cap":"(Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0527,0.5925,0.2186,0.6325]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","cap":"Left pulmonary artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5","ov":[[0.8314,0.3329,0.995,0.3871]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q15~left-pulmonary-artery","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right pulmonary artery","B. Posterior intercostal artery","C. Left pulmonary artery","D. Left main (primary) bronchus","E. Pulmonary veins"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left pulmonary artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left pulmonary artery.</b> The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Descending aorta behind; it arches over the left main bronchus</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left phrenic nerve in front; aortic arch above, tied to it by the ligamentum arteriosum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum under the aortic arch first; the pulmonary branch it attaches to, heading left over the left bronchus, is the left pulmonary artery.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>B. Posterior intercostal artery: The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below. <b>Comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk).</b><br>D. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>E. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The left main bronchus is usually inferior to the left pulmonary artery, while the right main bronchus is posterior to the right pulmonary artery."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.0073,0.3869,0.1584,0.45,"",0],[0.8299,0.4222,0.9927,0.4853,"",0],[0.0073,0.5633,0.1584,0.6264,"",0],[0.8299,0.267,0.9927,0.3301,"",1]],"ar":0.37474,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s005~left-pulmonary-artery"}]},{"l":"CAD-T2","u":4,"label":"Pulmonary veins","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_202_165.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary vessels","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.45","tb":1,"ov":[[0.1258,0.6287,0.2788,0.6894]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s005.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary veins on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 5","ov":[[0.005,0.4629,0.1783,0.5171],[0.8314,0.4993,0.995,0.5535],[0.005,0.5379,0.1783,0.5921],[0.8314,0.3989,0.995,0.4531]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q15~pulmonary-veins","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Pulmonary veins","B. Accessory hemiazygos vein","C. Left pulmonary artery","D. Left main (primary) bronchus","E. Right pulmonary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary veins","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Pulmonary veins.</b> The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Accessory hemiazygos vein: The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein. <b>Drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos).</b><br>C. Left pulmonary artery: The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk, arching over the left main bronchus as the highest structure in the left lung root. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right.</b><br>D. Left main (primary) bronchus: The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal than the right, passing under the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus to the left hilum.<br>E. Right pulmonary artery: The right pulmonary artery runs horizontally right from the pulmonary trunk, under the aortic arch and behind the ascending aorta and superior vena cava, in front of the right main bronchus. <b>Comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The left main bronchus is usually inferior to the left pulmonary artery, while the right main bronchus is posterior to the right pulmonary artery."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q15.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 15"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.0073,0.3869,0.1584,0.45,"",0],[0.8299,0.267,0.9927,0.3301,"",0],[0.8299,0.4222,0.9927,0.4853,"",1],[0.0073,0.5633,0.1584,0.6264,"",1]],"ar":0.37474,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s005~pulmonary-veins"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":5,"label":"Brachiocephalic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_192_1503.jpg","cap":"Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5014,0.1321,0.8688,0.1875]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s029~brachiocephalic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Brachiocephalic trunk","D. Aortopulmonary window","E. Ligamentum arteriosum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Brachiocephalic trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Brachiocephalic trunk.</b> The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right subclavian and right common carotid arteries, behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front; then the manubrium and thymus remnant</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left brachiocephalic vein and lift it; the large artery directly behind it, running from the arch up toward the right sternoclavicular joint, is the brachiocephalic trunk.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>B. Sympathetic trunk: The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes. <b>Comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes.</b><br>D. Aortopulmonary window: On frontal films the aortopulmonary window is the notch between the aortic knob and left pulmonary artery, holding the ligamentum arteriosum and left recurrent laryngeal nerve.<br>E. Ligamentum arteriosum: The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous cord from the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks lateral to it.<br>","ex":"","fl":["The brachiocephalic trunk splits into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries; the other two arch branches are the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s029.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 29"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9435,0.4219,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":0.81571}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":5,"label":"Ligamentum arteriosum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_193_1504.jpg","cap":"Great vessels and nerves","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.68","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6862,0.7584,0.8353,0.8639]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s092~ligamentum-arteriosum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ligamentum arteriosum","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Thoracic (descending) aorta","D. Brachiocephalic trunk","E. Vagus nerve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ligamentum arteriosum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ligamentum arteriosum.</b> The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous cord from the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks lateral to it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fetal ductus arteriosus, closed after birth</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Origin of the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Deep cardiac plexus and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks just behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the left vagus nerve crossing lateral to it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and follow it up to its bifurcation under the arch; the solid cord connecting that point to the arch is the ligamentum arteriosum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Sympathetic trunk: The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes. <b>Comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes.</b><br>C. Thoracic (descending) aorta: The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12. <b>Comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle).</b><br>D. Brachiocephalic trunk: The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid. <b>Comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium.</b><br>E. Vagus nerve: The vagus nerve descends in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and carotid artery, then passes behind the lung root to form the esophageal plexus. <b>Comes from medulla oblongata (cranial nerve X), leaving the skull through the jugular foramen.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s092.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 92"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.8946,0.4411,0.9836,"",1]],"ar":0.42214}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7509,0.2287,0.8677,0.2726]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q16.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 16","ov":[[0.1719,0.9204,0.3996,0.9912]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s027.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 27","ov":[[0.1719,0.9406,0.3489,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st23~phrenic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Posterior intercostal artery","C. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","D. Vagus nerve","E. Pericardiacophrenic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phrenic nerve (right/left).</b> Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>B. Posterior intercostal artery: The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below. <b>Comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk).</b><br>D. Vagus nerve: The vagus nerve descends in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and carotid artery, then passes behind the lung root to form the esophageal plexus. <b>Comes from medulla oblongata (cranial nerve X), leaving the skull through the jugular foramen.</b><br>E. Pericardiacophrenic artery: The pericardiacophrenic artery is a thin branch of the internal thoracic artery running with the phrenic nerve between the pericardium and mediastinal pleura, in front of the lung root. <b>Comes from internal thoracic artery, near the thoracic inlet.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st23.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 23"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.3636,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_179_1480.jpg","cap":"Cardiac nerves and plexus","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.63","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8246,0.1386,0.9492,0.1854]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st28~vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Thoracic duct","B. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","C. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Posterior intercostal artery","E. Vagus nerve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vagus nerve.</b> The vagus nerve descends in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and carotid artery, then passes behind the lung root to form the esophageal plexus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla oblongata (cranial nerve X), leaving the skull through the jugular foramen</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Larynx, heart, lungs and the gut to the left colic flexure (parasympathetic)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Trachea (right) or aortic arch (left), then the esophagus behind the lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Lung root in front of it; mediastinal pleura on its lateral side</dd></dl><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle. <b>Drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins).</b><br>B. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>C. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Posterior intercostal artery: The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below. <b>Comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st28.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 28"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.3636,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Right vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg","cap":"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7188,0.0717,0.8824,0.1127]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q01~right-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left vagus nerve","B. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","D. Right vagus nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right vagus nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right vagus nerve.</b> The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the posterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; azygos arch lateral to it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch of the azygos vein; the nerve passing medial to the arch on the side of the trachea and then behind the right lung root is the right vagus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>B. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve: The right recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the right vagus in front of the right subclavian artery, hooks under that artery and climbs in the groove between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from right vagus nerve, as it crosses in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>E. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q01.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 1"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9152,0.3826,0.9877,"",1]],"ar":0.53}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Left vagus nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_148_1418.jpg","cap":"Nerves of lungs and visceral pleura","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.40","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7747,0.0269,0.9316,0.1005]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s104~left-vagus-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Left vagus nerve","D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","E. Phrenic nerve (right/left)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left vagus nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left vagus nerve.</b> The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the anterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left side of the aortic arch; of the two nerves crossing it, follow the posterior one: if it gives a branch that hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, it is the left vagus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>E. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The probe lifts the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, the branch of the left vagus that hooks under the aortic arch."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s104.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 104"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9351,0.3676,0.9917,"",1]],"ar":0.70286}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1186,0.9564,0.161]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s102~left-recurrent-laryngeal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Right vagus nerve","C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","D. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","E. Left vagus nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve.</b> The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum, then the left tracheoesophageal groove</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum at its loop; left thyroid lobe higher up</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk bifurcation and the underside of the arch; the nerve curving under the arch just lateral to it is the left recurrent laryngeal.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve: The right recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the right vagus in front of the right subclavian artery, hooks under that artery and climbs in the groove between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from right vagus nerve, as it crosses in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>B. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>D. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>E. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s102.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 102"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9427,0.5004,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.80357}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7509,0.2287,0.8677,0.2726]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st23.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 23","ov":[[0.1795,0.9374,0.3656,0.9941]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s027.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 27","ov":[[0.1719,0.9406,0.3489,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q16~phrenic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left vagus nerve","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","D. Right vagus nerve","E. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phrenic nerve (right/left).</b> Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>E. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q16.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 16"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9224,0.3976,0.9892,"",1]],"ar":0.58143,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st23~phrenic-nerve"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":6,"label":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg","cap":"Costal surfaces of lungs","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7509,0.2287,0.8677,0.2726]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st23.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 23","ov":[[0.1795,0.9374,0.3656,0.9941]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q16.jpg","cap":"Phrenic nerve (right/left) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 16","ov":[[0.1719,0.9204,0.3996,0.9912]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s027~phrenic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right vagus nerve","B. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","C. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Left vagus nerve","E. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Phrenic nerve (right/left)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Phrenic nerve (right/left).</b> Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>C. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>E. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s027.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 27"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9426,0.3469,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.80143,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st23~phrenic-nerve"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":7,"label":"Thoracic (descending) aorta","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0079,0.7036,0.1558,0.7672]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s100~thoracic-aorta","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Thoracic (descending) aorta","D. Posterior intercostal artery","E. Thoracic duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic (descending) aorta","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thoracic (descending) aorta.</b> The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior intercostal (3rd–11th), subcostal, bronchial and esophageal arteries; becomes abdominal aorta at T12</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies; hemiazygos veins cross behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Pull the left lung forward and look behind its root; the large artery on the left side of the vertebral column, with paired intercostal branches, is the thoracic aorta.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Posterior intercostal artery: The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below. <b>Comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk).</b><br>E. Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle. <b>Drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s100.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 100"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.4661,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.8}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":7,"label":"Posterior intercostal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0154,0.4125,0.1816,0.4939]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s021~posterior-intercostal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior intercostal artery","B. Posterior intercostal vein","C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","D. Thoracic (descending) aorta","E. Left vagus nerve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior intercostal artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Posterior intercostal artery.</b> The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Intercostal space and overlying skin; dorsal branch to back muscles and spinal cord</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle, in the costal groove of the rib above</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the descending thoracic aorta first; the paired small branches leaving its back at each level and running into the intercostal spaces are the posterior intercostal arteries.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Posterior intercostal vein: Each posterior intercostal vein runs highest in the costal groove, above its artery and nerve, draining into the azygos vein on the right or hemiazygos veins on the left. <b>Drains into azygos vein (right); hemiazygos or accessory hemiazygos (left); upper spaces to superior intercostal veins.</b><br>C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>D. Thoracic (descending) aorta: The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12. <b>Comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle).</b><br>E. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s021.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 21"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9433,0.4719,0.9934,"",1]],"ar":0.81214}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Hemiazygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_200_1515.jpg","cap":"Azygos system of veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.74","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6063,0.4533,0.8654,0.5023]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s003.jpg","cap":"Hemiazygos vein on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 3","ov":[[0.1719,0.9298,0.3731,0.9931]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q03~hemiazygos-vein","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Azygos vein","B. Thoracic duct","C. Hemiazygos vein","D. Middle cardiac vein","E. Right vagus nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemiazygos vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hemiazygos vein.</b> The hemiazygos vein climbs the left side of the lower thoracic vertebrae, then crosses right at T9 behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct to join the azygos.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins, plus the lowest left posterior intercostals</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein on the right; look for a vein joining it from the left across the front of the lower vertebral bodies behind the aorta, and follow it back to the left side.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Azygos vein: The azygos vein climbs the right side of the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, then arches over the right main bronchus at T4 into the superior vena cava. <b>Drains into back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4.</b><br>B. Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle. <b>Drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins).</b><br>D. Middle cardiac vein: The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus, near its right end.</b><br>E. Right vagus nerve: The right vagus nerve runs down the right side of the trachea, medial to the azygos arch, then passes behind the right lung root onto the esophagus. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins cross behind the aorta to drain into the azygos vein."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q03.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 3"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.7913,0.6112,0.993,0.6906,"",0],[0.007,0.5077,0.1363,0.5871,"",1]],"ar":0.28316}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Accessory hemiazygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_184_141.jpg","cap":"Veins of the thoracic wall","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.30","tb":1,"ov":[[0.768,0.2147,0.9946,0.2666]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q03~accessory-hemiazygos-vein","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Azygos vein","B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","C. Thoracic duct","D. Accessory hemiazygos vein","E. Pulmonary veins"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Accessory hemiazygos vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Accessory hemiazygos vein.</b> The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces; sometimes left bronchial veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta and thoracic duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein, then look for veins joining it from the left: the higher crossing (about T7–T8), fed from the upper left intercostal spaces, is the accessory hemiazygos.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Azygos vein: The azygos vein climbs the right side of the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, then arches over the right main bronchus at T4 into the superior vena cava. <b>Drains into back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4.</b><br>B. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>C. Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle. <b>Drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins).</b><br>E. Pulmonary veins: The pulmonary veins, two from each lung, are the lowest and most anterior structures in each lung root, opening into the back of the left atrium. <b>Drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins cross behind the aorta to drain into the azygos vein."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q03.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 3"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.5077,0.1363,0.5871,"",0],[0.7913,0.6112,0.993,0.6906,"",1]],"ar":0.28316}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Thoracic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1827,0.825,0.2248]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s019.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 19","ov":[[0.1719,0.9073,0.3489,0.9885]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q02.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 2","ov":[[0.1719,0.9073,0.3489,0.9885]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st22~thoracic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Thoracic (descending) aorta","C. Accessory hemiazygos vein","D. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","E. Thoracic duct"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic duct","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Thoracic duct.</b> The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>B. Thoracic (descending) aorta: The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12. <b>Comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle).</b><br>C. Accessory hemiazygos vein: The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein. <b>Drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos).</b><br>D. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st22.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 22"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9393,0.3636,0.992,"",1]],"ar":0.80643}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Hemiazygos vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_200_1515.jpg","cap":"Azygos system of veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.74","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6063,0.4533,0.8654,0.5023]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q03.jpg","cap":"Hemiazygos vein on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 3","ov":[[0.005,0.5057,0.1383,0.5891]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s003~hemiazygos-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","B. Thoracic duct","C. Middle cardiac vein","D. Azygos vein","E. Hemiazygos vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemiazygos vein","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hemiazygos vein.</b> The hemiazygos vein climbs the left side of the lower thoracic vertebrae, then crosses right at T9 behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct to join the azygos.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins, plus the lowest left posterior intercostals</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein on the right; look for a vein joining it from the left across the front of the lower vertebral bodies behind the aorta, and follow it back to the left side.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>B. Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle. <b>Drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins).</b><br>C. Middle cardiac vein: The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus, near its right end.</b><br>D. Azygos vein: The azygos vein climbs the right side of the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, then arches over the right main bronchus at T4 into the superior vena cava. <b>Drains into back of the SVC (superior vena cava), after arching over the right lung root at T4.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s003.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 3"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9318,0.3711,0.9911,"",1]],"ar":0.66643,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q03~hemiazygos-vein"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Thoracic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1827,0.825,0.2248]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st22.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 22","ov":[[0.1795,0.9373,0.3656,0.994]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q02.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 2","ov":[[0.1719,0.9073,0.3489,0.9885]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s019~thoracic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Thoracic (descending) aorta","C. Thoracic duct","D. Brachiocephalic trunk","E. Accessory hemiazygos vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic duct","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thoracic duct.</b> The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>B. Thoracic (descending) aorta: The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12. <b>Comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle).</b><br>D. Brachiocephalic trunk: The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid. <b>Comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium.</b><br>E. Accessory hemiazygos vein: The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein. <b>Drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s019.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 19"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9093,0.3469,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49357,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st22~thoracic-duct"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":8,"label":"Thoracic duct","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg","cap":"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6974,0.1827,0.825,0.2248]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st22.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 22","ov":[[0.1795,0.9373,0.3656,0.994]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s019.jpg","cap":"Thoracic duct on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 19","ov":[[0.1719,0.9073,0.3489,0.9885]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q02~thoracic-duct","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Thoracic (descending) aorta","B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","C. Thoracic duct","D. Ligamentum arteriosum","E. Accessory hemiazygos vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic duct","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thoracic duct.</b> The thoracic duct ascends on the vertebral bodies behind the esophagus, between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, crossing left at T4–T6 to the left venous angle.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Thoracic (descending) aorta: The descending thoracic aorta continues the arch at T4, running down the left side of the vertebral bodies behind the left lung root to the aortic hiatus at T12. <b>Comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle).</b><br>B. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>D. Ligamentum arteriosum: The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous cord from the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks lateral to it.<br>E. Accessory hemiazygos vein: The accessory hemiazygos vein runs down the left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies, draining upper left intercostal veins, then crosses behind the aorta to the azygos vein. <b>Drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q02.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 2"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9093,0.3469,0.9865,"",1]],"ar":0.49357,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st22~thoracic-duct"}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":9,"label":"Sympathetic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg","cap":"Innervation of small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0158,0.052,0.2201,0.0932]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q04.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic trunk on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 4","ov":[[0.1719,0.9074,0.386,0.9886]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st21~sympathetic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","B. Sympathetic trunk","C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","D. Brachiocephalic trunk","E. Posterior intercostal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic trunk","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sympathetic trunk.</b> The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>C. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: The left recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the left vagus at the aortic arch, hooks under it lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, and climbs between trachea and esophagus. <b>Comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch.</b><br>D. Brachiocephalic trunk: The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid. <b>Comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium.</b><br>E. Posterior intercostal artery: The posterior intercostal arteries leave the back of the thoracic aorta at each level and run in the costal groove between the vein above and nerve below. <b>Comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st21.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 21"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9393,0.4074,0.992,"",1]],"ar":0.80643}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":9,"label":"Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_423_467.jpg","cap":"Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.158","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6661,0.6678,0.8699,0.7053]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s106~greater-splanchnic-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Sympathetic trunk","B. Phrenic nerve (right/left)","C. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","D. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","E. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve.</b> The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Celiac ganglion (foregut), after piercing the crus; some fibers reach the suprarenal medulla</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the sympathetic trunk on the rib heads, then follow the medial branches from its middle ganglia as they join into one nerve heading down onto the vertebral bodies.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Sympathetic trunk: The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes. <b>Comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes.</b><br>B. Phrenic nerve (right/left): Each phrenic nerve runs down the fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; the right follows the superior vena cava, the left crosses the aortic arch. <b>Comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4).</b><br>C. Lesser (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The lesser splanchnic nerve leaves the T10 and T11 sympathetic ganglia, descends lateral to the greater splanchnic nerve, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the aorticorenal ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T10–T11.</b><br>E. Least (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The least splanchnic nerve is a small branch of the T12 sympathetic ganglion, below the lesser splanchnic nerve, that ends in the renal plexus. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglion T12 (small and inconstant).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s106.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 106"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.4547,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.80071}]},{"l":"CAD-T3","u":9,"label":"Sympathetic trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg","cap":"Innervation of small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0158,0.052,0.2201,0.0932]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st21.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic trunk on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 21","ov":[[0.1795,0.9373,0.4094,0.994]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q04~sympathetic-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Brachiocephalic trunk","B. Left vagus nerve","C. Sympathetic trunk","D. Ligamentum arteriosum","E. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Sympathetic trunk.</b> The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Brachiocephalic trunk: The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid. <b>Comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium.</b><br>B. Left vagus nerve: The left vagus nerve descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the aortic arch behind the left phrenic, and passes behind the left lung root. <b>Comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries.</b><br>D. Ligamentum arteriosum: The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous cord from the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks lateral to it.<br>E. Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: The greater splanchnic nerve forms from T5–T9 sympathetic ganglion branches, descends on the sides of the vertebral bodies, and pierces the diaphragmatic crus to reach the celiac ganglion. <b>Comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The sympathetic ganglia sit along the trunk like beads."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q04.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 4"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9094,0.384,0.9866,"",1]],"ar":0.49429,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st21~sympathetic-trunk"}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":10,"label":"Posterior interventricular sulcus","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_214_186.jpg","cap":"Diaphragmatic surface of the heart","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.5019,0.9493,0.7671,0.9906]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q17~posterior-interventricular-sulcus","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Crista terminalis","B. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","D. Posterior interventricular sulcus","E. Interventricular septum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior interventricular sulcus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Posterior interventricular sulcus.</b> The posterior interventricular sulcus is the groove on the heart's underside between the ventricles, running from crux to apex with the posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior part of the interventricular septum, deep to the groove</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein lie in it; diaphragm beneath</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the heart and look at the surface that rested on the diaphragm; follow the coronary sinus to the crux, and the groove running from there toward the apex is the posterior interventricular sulcus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Crista terminalis: The crista terminalis is the ridge inside the right atrium's lateral wall, running from superior to inferior vena cava, with pectinate muscles in front and smooth wall behind.<br>B. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>E. Interventricular septum: The interventricular septum is the thick muscular wall between the right and left ventricles, lying deep to the interventricular grooves and bulging into the right ventricle.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q17.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 17"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9232,0.5269,0.9893,"",1]],"ar":0.58786}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":11,"label":"Superior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_195_1508.jpg","cap":"Lateral exposures of mediastinum","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7935,0.5031,0.9471,0.5427]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q19~superior-vena-cava","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Inferior vena cava","B. Ascending aorta","C. Papillary muscle","D. Pulmonary trunk","E. Superior vena cava"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior vena cava","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Superior vena cava.</b> The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left brachiocephalic veins, joining behind the right 1st costal cartilage; receives the azygos</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery and right superior pulmonary vein behind; right vagus and trachea higher up</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right phrenic nerve and pleura on its right; thymus and right lung edge in front</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ascending aorta inside the pericardium; the thin-walled vein on its right side running down into the right atrium is the SVC.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Inferior vena cava: The inferior vena cava rises just right of the aorta, passes behind the duodenum, pancreatic head and liver, and pierces the diaphragm at T8 into the right atrium. <b>Drains into right atrium, lower part, after piercing the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8.</b><br>B. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right. <b>Comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve.</b><br>C. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>D. Pulmonary trunk: The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle in front of and left of the ascending aorta and divides under the aortic arch into the two pulmonary arteries. <b>Comes from right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q19.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 19"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0098,0.7354,0.188,0.8086,"",0],[0.0098,0.6579,0.188,0.731,"",0],[0.0098,0.5803,0.188,0.6535,"",1]],"ar":0.38797}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":11,"label":"Pulmonary trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg","cap":"Interior of right ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.008,0.087,0.1549,0.1358]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s009.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary trunk on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 9","ov":[[0.1731,0.9489,0.373,0.9967]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q19~pulmonary-trunk","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Superior vena cava","B. Ascending aorta","C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","D. Middle cardiac vein","E. Pulmonary trunk"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary trunk","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Pulmonary trunk.</b> The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle in front of and left of the ascending aorta and divides under the aortic arch into the two pulmonary arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left pulmonary arteries, dividing under the aortic arch at sternal-angle level</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Ascending aorta to its right; transverse pericardial sinus and left atrium behind</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; left auricle curls around its left side</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the most anterior great vessel leaving the front of the heart, to the left of the ascending aorta; follow it up to where it forks under the arch.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein. <b>Drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage.</b><br>B. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right. <b>Comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve.</b><br>C. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>D. Middle cardiac vein: The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus, near its right end.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q19.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 19"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0098,0.5803,0.188,0.6535,"",0],[0.0098,0.7354,0.188,0.8086,"",0],[0.0098,0.6579,0.188,0.731,"",1]],"ar":0.38797}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":11,"label":"Ascending aorta","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1356.jpg","cap":"Arteries of thoracic wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0147,0.2798,0.2359,0.3268]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q19~ascending-aorta","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Papillary muscle","B. Ascending aorta","C. Aortic valve","D. Superior vena cava","E. Pulmonary trunk"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending aorta","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ascending aorta.</b> The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left ventricle, through the aortic valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left coronary arteries (its only branches); continues as the arch at the sternal angle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root; then pericardium and sternum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk leaving the front of the heart; the larger vessel just to its right, between it and the superior vena cava, is the ascending aorta.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>C. Aortic valve: The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and ascending aorta, behind the sternum at the third intercostal space, behind and right of the pulmonary valve.<br>D. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein. <b>Drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage.</b><br>E. Pulmonary trunk: The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle in front of and left of the ascending aorta and divides under the aortic arch into the two pulmonary arteries. <b>Comes from right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q19.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 19"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0098,0.5803,0.188,0.6535,"",0],[0.0098,0.6579,0.188,0.731,"",0],[0.0098,0.7354,0.188,0.8086,"",1]],"ar":0.38797}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":11,"label":"Pulmonary trunk","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg","cap":"Interior of right ventricle of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54","tb":1,"ov":[[0.008,0.087,0.1549,0.1358]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q19.jpg","cap":"Pulmonary trunk on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 19","ov":[[0.0078,0.6559,0.19,0.733]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s009~pulmonary-trunk","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior vena cava","B. Ascending aorta","C. Brachiocephalic trunk","D. Sympathetic trunk","E. Pulmonary trunk"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pulmonary trunk","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Pulmonary trunk.</b> The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle in front of and left of the ascending aorta and divides under the aortic arch into the two pulmonary arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left pulmonary arteries, dividing under the aortic arch at sternal-angle level</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Ascending aorta to its right; transverse pericardial sinus and left atrium behind</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; left auricle curls around its left side</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the most anterior great vessel leaving the front of the heart, to the left of the ascending aorta; follow it up to where it forks under the arch.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein. <b>Drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage.</b><br>B. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right. <b>Comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve.</b><br>C. Brachiocephalic trunk: The brachiocephalic trunk rises from the arch behind the left brachiocephalic vein, right of the trachea, splitting behind the right sternoclavicular joint into right subclavian and common carotid. <b>Comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium.</b><br>D. Sympathetic trunk: The sympathetic trunk is the ganglion chain running down over the rib heads behind the costal pleura, linked to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes. <b>Comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s009.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 9"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1751,0.9509,0.371,0.9947,"",1]],"ar":0.96786,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S06-q19~pulmonary-trunk"}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":12,"label":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1474.jpg","cap":"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0301,0.721,0.3788,0.8856]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q18.jpg","cap":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 18","ov":[[0.1719,0.9247,0.511,0.9921]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st27~anterior-interventricular-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Posterior left ventricular artery","B. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","C. Interventricular septal branches","D. Lateral (diagonal) artery","E. Circumflex artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD).</b> The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior two-thirds of septum, front of both ventricles and apex, via diagonal and septal branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; great cardiac vein runs beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle; trace the left coronary artery from between them to the branch that turns straight down the front of the heart.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Posterior left ventricular artery: On a coronary angiogram the posterior left ventricular artery runs over the diaphragmatic surface of the left ventricle, coming from the right coronary past the crux in right-dominant hearts. <b>Comes from right coronary artery beyond the crux (right-dominant); circumflex in left-dominant hearts.</b><br>C. Interventricular septal branches: On a coronary angiogram the septal branches are short, straight vessels leaving the anterior and posterior interventricular arteries at right angles to plunge into the interventricular septum. <b>Comes from anterior interventricular artery (front two-thirds) and posterior interventricular artery (back third).</b><br>D. Lateral (diagonal) artery: On a coronary angiogram the lateral (diagonal) arteries branch off the left anterior descending artery and run diagonally over the front of the left ventricle toward its lateral wall. <b>Comes from anterior interventricular artery (LAD, left anterior descending).</b><br>E. Circumflex artery: The circumflex artery leaves the left coronary artery and runs left under the left auricle in the coronary groove, beside the great cardiac vein, onto the heart's back. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st27.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 27"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2205,0.9503,0.6475,0.9944,"",1]],"ar":1.21414}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":12,"label":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1474.jpg","cap":"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0301,0.721,0.3788,0.8856]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st27.jpg","cap":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD) on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 27","ov":[[0.2185,0.9483,0.6495,0.9964]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q18~anterior-interventricular-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","B. Lateral (diagonal) artery","C. Circumflex artery","D. Left marginal artery","E. Left coronary artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Anterior interventricular artery (LAD).</b> The anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery branches from the left coronary artery and runs down the anterior interventricular groove to the apex beside the great cardiac vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior two-thirds of septum, front of both ventricles and apex, via diagonal and septal branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; great cardiac vein runs beside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle; trace the left coronary artery from between them to the branch that turns straight down the front of the heart.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Lateral (diagonal) artery: On a coronary angiogram the lateral (diagonal) arteries branch off the left anterior descending artery and run diagonally over the front of the left ventricle toward its lateral wall. <b>Comes from anterior interventricular artery (LAD, left anterior descending).</b><br>C. Circumflex artery: The circumflex artery leaves the left coronary artery and runs left under the left auricle in the coronary groove, beside the great cardiac vein, onto the heart's back. <b>Comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches.</b><br>D. Left marginal artery: The left marginal artery is a branch of the circumflex artery that runs down the left (obtuse) border of the heart over the left ventricle toward the apex. <b>Comes from circumflex artery.</b><br>E. Left coronary artery: The left coronary artery leaves the left aortic sinus and runs between the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle before splitting into the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries. <b>Comes from left aortic sinus of the ascending aorta.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q18.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 18"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9267,0.509,0.9901,"",1]],"ar":0.61786,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st27~anterior-interventricular-artery"}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":13,"label":"Middle cardiac vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg","cap":"Cardiac veins","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8845,0.808,0.9874,0.9114]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s007~middle-cardiac-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Middle cardiac vein","B. Hemiazygos vein","C. Great cardiac vein","D. Anterior cardiac veins","E. Small cardiac vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle cardiac vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Middle cardiac vein.</b> The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Diaphragmatic surfaces of both ventricles, from near the apex</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, near its right end</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; diaphragm below the heart</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus on the back of the heart; the vein entering it from the groove that runs down toward the apex is the middle cardiac vein.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Hemiazygos vein: The hemiazygos vein climbs the left side of the lower thoracic vertebrae, then crosses right at T9 behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct to join the azygos. <b>Drains into azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9.</b><br>C. Great cardiac vein: The great cardiac vein climbs the anterior interventricular groove beside the anterior interventricular artery, then turns left in the coronary groove to become the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus, where the oblique vein of the left atrium joins on the back of the heart.</b><br>D. Anterior cardiac veins: The anterior cardiac veins are small veins on the front of the right ventricle that cross the right coronary artery and open directly into the right atrium. <b>Drains into right atrium directly, not the coronary sinus.</b><br>E. Small cardiac vein: The small cardiac vein runs with the right marginal artery along the heart's inferior border, then back in the right coronary groove to end in the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus near its right end (sometimes the middle cardiac vein).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s007.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 7"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9366,0.394,0.992,"",1]],"ar":0.72071}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":14,"label":"Crista terminalis","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_168_1458.jpg","cap":"Right atrium of heart","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.53","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0057,0.5943,0.147,0.6418]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s013~crista-terminalis","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Crista terminalis","B. Papillary muscle","C. Pectinate muscles","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Aortic valve"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Crista terminalis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Crista terminalis.</b> The crista terminalis is the ridge inside the right atrium's lateral wall, running from superior to inferior vena cava, with pectinate muscles in front and smooth wall behind.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Lateral right atrial wall, under the sulcus terminalis; SA (sinoatrial) node at its top</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium only, facing the right atrial cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the two vena caval openings; the ridge running between them along the lateral wall, where rough wall meets smooth wall, is the crista terminalis.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>C. Pectinate muscles: The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<br>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>E. Aortic valve: The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and ascending aorta, behind the sternum at the third intercostal space, behind and right of the pulmonary valve.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s013.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 13"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.934,0.3654,0.9915,"",1]],"ar":0.69}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":14,"label":"Pectinate muscles","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_442_1973.jpg","cap":"Transitions occurring at pectinate line","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0292,0.604,0.9822,0.6624]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s090.jpg","cap":"Pectinate muscles on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 90","ov":[[0.1719,0.9452,0.3867,0.9962]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st25~pectinate-muscles","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","B. Pectinate muscles","C. Papillary muscle","D. Crista terminalis","E. Superior vena cava"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pectinate muscles","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pectinate muscles.</b> The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the crista terminalis; the ridges running forward from it across the anterior wall and into the auricle are the pectinate muscles.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>C. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>D. Crista terminalis: The crista terminalis is the ridge inside the right atrium's lateral wall, running from superior to inferior vena cava, with pectinate muscles in front and smooth wall behind.<br>E. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein. <b>Drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st25.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 25"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2403,0.9508,0.5252,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.33525}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":14,"label":"Pectinate muscles","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_442_1973.jpg","cap":"Transitions occurring at pectinate line","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0292,0.604,0.9822,0.6624]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st25.jpg","cap":"Pectinate muscles on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 25","ov":[[0.2383,0.9488,0.5272,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s090~pectinate-muscles","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Papillary muscle","B. Middle cardiac vein","C. Crista terminalis","D. Ascending aorta","E. Pectinate muscles"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pectinate muscles","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Pectinate muscles.</b> The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the crista terminalis; the ridges running forward from it across the anterior wall and into the auricle are the pectinate muscles.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>B. Middle cardiac vein: The middle cardiac vein climbs in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the right end of the coronary sinus. <b>Drains into coronary sinus, near its right end.</b><br>C. Crista terminalis: The crista terminalis is the ridge inside the right atrium's lateral wall, running from superior to inferior vena cava, with pectinate muscles in front and smooth wall behind.<br>D. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right. <b>Comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s090.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 90"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9472,0.3847,0.9942,"",1]],"ar":0.87786,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st25~pectinate-muscles"}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":15,"label":"Trabeculae carneae","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4411,0.9498,0.622,0.9898]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q11.jpg","cap":"Trabeculae carneae on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 11","ov":[[0.1719,0.9466,0.4003,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s098~trabeculae-carneae","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Trabeculae carneae","B. Chordae tendineae","C. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","D. Papillary muscle","E. Ascending aorta"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Trabeculae carneae","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Trabeculae carneae.</b> The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle; the rough, interlacing muscle on the walls below the valves, apart from the large papillary muscles, is the trabeculae carneae.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Chordae tendineae: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<br>C. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>D. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>E. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta rises up and right from the aortic valve inside the pericardium, between the pulmonary trunk on its left and superior vena cava on its right. <b>Comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s098.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 98"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.943,0.3983,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.80786}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":15,"label":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7714,0.7042,0.9939,0.7446]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s096~septomarginal-trabecula","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Trabeculae carneae","B. Pectinate muscles","C. Chordae tendineae","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Papillary muscle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band).</b> The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Trabeculae carneae: The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<br>B. Pectinate muscles: The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<br>C. Chordae tendineae: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<br>E. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s096.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 96"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9422,0.4469,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.79643}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":15,"label":"Papillary muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the left ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0062,0.5785,0.1699,0.6198]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st24~papillary-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Trabeculae carneae","B. Papillary muscle","C. Chordae tendineae","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Pectinate muscles"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Papillary muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Papillary muscle.</b> Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; left: anterior, posterior)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Chordae tendineae, to the cusps of the tricuspid or mitral valve</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium; chordae tendineae attach at its tip</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle and follow the chordae tendineae down from a valve cusp; the muscle mound they attach to is a papillary muscle.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Trabeculae carneae: The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<br>C. Chordae tendineae: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<br>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>E. Pectinate muscles: The pectinate muscles are the parallel muscular ridges lining the inside of the right atrium's anterior wall and auricle, in front of the crista terminalis.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st24.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 24"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.187,0.9449,0.3913,0.9937,"",1]],"ar":0.89877}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":15,"label":"Chordae tendineae","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the left ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0912,0.4204,0.2682,0.4617]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s094~chordae-tendineae","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Aortic valve","B. Trabeculae carneae","C. Papillary muscle","D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","E. Chordae tendineae"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Chordae tendineae","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Chordae tendineae.</b> The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fibrous collagen cords covered by endocardium</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Papillary muscle tips to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of tricuspid and mitral cusps</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle, find a cusp of the atrioventricular valve, and follow the threads hanging from its edge down to a papillary muscle.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Aortic valve: The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and ascending aorta, behind the sternum at the third intercostal space, behind and right of the pulmonary valve.<br>B. Trabeculae carneae: The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<br>C. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>D. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s094.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 94"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2414,0.9552,0.538,0.9954,"",1]],"ar":1.48558}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":15,"label":"Trabeculae carneae","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg","cap":"Internal view of the right ventricle","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4411,0.9498,0.622,0.9898]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s098.jpg","cap":"Trabeculae carneae on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 98","ov":[[0.1719,0.941,0.4003,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q11~trabeculae-carneae","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior vena cava","B. Trabeculae carneae","C. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","D. Papillary muscle","E. Chordae tendineae"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Trabeculae carneae","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Trabeculae carneae.</b> The trabeculae carneae are the rough muscular ridges lining the inner walls of both ventricles below the valves, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle; the rough, interlacing muscle on the walls below the valves, apart from the large papillary muscles, is the trabeculae carneae.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava forms behind the right first costal cartilage from the two brachiocephalic veins and runs down right of the ascending aorta into the right atrium, receiving the azygos vein. <b>Drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage.</b><br>C. Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band): The septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) is the muscular band crossing the right ventricle from the lower interventricular septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, carrying the right bundle branch.<br>D. Papillary muscle: Papillary muscles are cone-shaped muscle columns rising from the inner ventricular walls, their tips tied by chordae tendineae to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves.<br>E. Chordae tendineae: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q11.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 11"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9486,0.3983,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":0.90286,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s098~trabeculae-carneae"}]},{"l":"CAD-T4","u":16,"label":"Aortic valve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg","cap":"Valves of heart and great vessels","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55","tb":1,"ov":[[0.797,0.6072,0.9005,0.6455]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st26~aortic-valve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve","B. Cusp of the mitral valve","C. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve","D. Pulmonary valve","E. Aortic valve"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Aortic valve","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Aortic valve.</b> The aortic valve lies between the left ventricle and ascending aorta, behind the sternum at the third intercostal space, behind and right of the pulmonary valve.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it; anterior mitral cusp continuous with its base</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front; left sternum at the 3rd intercostal space</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the ascending aorta just above the heart and look down; the three cusps at its base, with coronary openings above two of them, form the aortic valve.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Tricuspid (right atrioventricular) valve: The tricuspid valve guards the opening from the right atrium into the right ventricle, its three cusps tied by chordae tendineae to papillary muscles, behind the lower sternal body.<br>B. Cusp of the mitral valve: The two mitral valve cusps, anterior and posterior, hang from the opening between the left atrium and left ventricle, tied by chordae tendineae to the papillary muscles.<br>C. Mitral (bicuspid; left atrioventricular) valve: The mitral valve is the two-cusp valve between the left atrium and left ventricle, tied by chordae tendineae to two papillary muscles, its anterior cusp beside the aortic opening.<br>D. Pulmonary valve: The pulmonary valve is the three-cusp semilunar valve at the top of the right ventricle's outflow tract, in front of, above and left of the aortic valve.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st26.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 26"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1817,0.9394,0.3638,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A1","u":17,"label":"Internal oblique muscle","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg","cap":"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6","tb":1,"ov":[[0.4049,0.0569,0.5834,0.1056]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q10~internal-oblique-muscle","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Transversus abdominis muscle","B. External oblique muscle","C. Aponeurosis of external oblique","D. Internal oblique muscle","E. Inferior epigastric artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique muscle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Internal oblique muscle.</b> The internal oblique is the middle flat abdominal muscle, between the external oblique outside and transversus abdominis inside, with fibres running up and toward the midline.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Transversus abdominis muscle: In axial sections transversus abdominis is the innermost of the three flat lateral muscles, deep to internal oblique and just outside the transversalis fascia and peritoneum.<br>B. External oblique muscle: In axial sections the external oblique is the outermost flat muscle of the lateral abdominal wall, superficial to internal oblique and transversus abdominis.<br>C. Aponeurosis of external oblique: In axial sections the external oblique aponeurosis is the thin white sheet continuing the muscle medially, passing in front of rectus abdominis to the linea alba.<br>E. Inferior epigastric artery: The inferior epigastric artery leaves the external iliac above the inguinal ligament, climbs medial to the deep inguinal ring, and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line. <b>Comes from external iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q10.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 10"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9342,0.5469,0.9916,"",1]],"ar":0.69286}]},{"l":"CAD-A1","u":18,"label":"Inferior epigastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_376_1869.jpg","cap":"Pelvic fascial ligaments","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.14","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7076,0.1607,0.8425,0.2073]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st32~inferior-epigastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ilioinguinal nerve","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Common hepatic artery","E. Splenic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Inferior epigastric artery.</b> The inferior epigastric artery leaves the external iliac above the inguinal ligament, climbs medial to the deep inguinal ring, and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>External iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lower rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> From inside the lower abdominal wall, find the deep inguinal ring with the ductus deferens or round ligament entering it; the vessel climbing along its medial edge toward rectus is the inferior epigastric artery.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Ilioinguinal nerve: The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs across quadratus lumborum just below the iliohypogastric nerve, then through the inguinal canal beside the spermatic cord, exiting the superficial inguinal ring. <b>Comes from L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve).</b><br>B. Superior epigastric artery: The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, entering the rectus sheath below the 6th intercostal space and running down behind rectus abdominis. <b>Comes from internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch).</b><br>D. Common hepatic artery: The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries. <b>Comes from celiac trunk.</b><br>E. Splenic artery: The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st32.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 32"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.4698,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A1","u":18,"label":"Ilioinguinal nerve","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg","cap":"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0272,0.3236,0.1753,0.3616]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st31~ilioinguinal-nerve","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Inferior epigastric artery","B. Ilioinguinal nerve","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Internal oblique muscle","E. Superior epigastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ilioinguinal nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ilioinguinal nerve.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs across quadratus lumborum just below the iliohypogastric nerve, then through the inguinal canal beside the spermatic cord, exiting the superficial inguinal ring.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Inferior epigastric artery: The inferior epigastric artery leaves the external iliac above the inguinal ligament, climbs medial to the deep inguinal ring, and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line. <b>Comes from external iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament.</b><br>C. Inguinal ligament: The inguinal ligament is the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, running from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle over the femoral vessels.<br>D. Internal oblique muscle: The internal oblique is the middle flat abdominal muscle, between the external oblique outside and transversus abdominis inside, with fibres running up and toward the midline.<br>E. Superior epigastric artery: The superior epigastric artery is the medial terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery, entering the rectus sheath below the 6th intercostal space and running down behind rectus abdominis. <b>Comes from internal thoracic artery (its medial terminal branch).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st31.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.3917,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":19,"label":"Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_328_1762.jpg","cap":"","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0475,0.6131,0.3326,0.7337]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q06~inferior-part-of-duodenum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior (1st) part of duodenum","B. Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum","C. Spleen","D. Descending (2nd) part of duodenum","E. Ascending (4th) part of duodenum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum.</b> The third part of the duodenum crosses L3 from right to left, between the superior mesenteric vessels in front and the inferior vena cava and aorta behind.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right psoas major, IVC (inferior vena cava), aorta and right ureter, at L3</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior mesenteric artery and vein, and the root of the mesentery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMA and SMV descending into the mesentery; the transverse loop of bowel passing behind them, in front of the aorta, is the third part.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior (1st) part of duodenum: The superior (first) part of the duodenum runs right and back from the pylorus at L1, with the bile duct, gastroduodenal artery and portal vein behind it.<br>C. Spleen: The spleen sits in the left upper abdomen against the diaphragm at ribs 9–11, behind and left of the stomach, lateral to the left kidney and above the left colic flexure.<br>D. Descending (2nd) part of duodenum: The descending (second) part of the duodenum runs down beside L1–L3, around the pancreatic head, in front of the right kidney and right of the inferior vena cava.<br>E. Ascending (4th) part of duodenum: The ascending (fourth) part of the duodenum rises along the left side of the aorta on left psoas to L2, turning forward as the duodenojejunal flexure.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q06.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 6"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9286,0.4361,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":0.635}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":19,"label":"Spleen","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_272_1644.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of duodenum, pancreas, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.46","tb":1,"ov":[[0.9069,0.2237,0.9915,0.2657]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s037~spleen","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Tail of pancreas","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Descending (2nd) part of duodenum","D. Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum","E. Spleen"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Spleen.</b> The spleen sits in the left upper abdomen against the diaphragm at ribs 9–11, behind and left of the stomach, lateral to the left kidney and above the left colic flexure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally; left kidney medially</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach anteriorly (gastric impression); left colic flexure below</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the fundus of the stomach and follow the gastrosplenic ligament laterally to the solid organ tucked under the left dome of the diaphragm.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Tail of pancreas: The tail is the narrow left end of the pancreas, lying in front of the left kidney and running with the splenic vessels in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum.<br>B. Common hepatic artery: The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries. <b>Comes from celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Descending (2nd) part of duodenum: The descending (second) part of the duodenum runs down beside L1–L3, around the pancreatic head, in front of the right kidney and right of the inferior vena cava.<br>D. Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum: The third part of the duodenum crosses L3 from right to left, between the superior mesenteric vessels in front and the inferior vena cava and aorta behind.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s037.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 37"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9414,0.3469,0.993,"",1]],"ar":0.78429}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":20,"label":"Left gastric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg","cap":"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0836,0.9464,0.2303,0.9874]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s017~left-gastric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Splenic artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Right gastric artery","E. Left gastric artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left gastric artery.</b> The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, climbing to the esophagogastric junction and then running down the lesser curvature inside the lesser omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its smallest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of the stomach and the abdominal esophagus; joins the right gastric artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then take the branch that heads up to the esophagogastric junction before curving down the lesser curvature.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Splenic artery: The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch).</b><br>B. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>C. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum.</b><br>D. Right gastric artery: The right gastric artery, a branch of the proper hepatic artery, runs left along the lesser curvature from the pylorus in the lesser omentum to join the left gastric artery. <b>Comes from proper hepatic artery.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s017.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 17"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9428,0.379,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.805}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":21,"label":"Splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_296_1694.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3944,0.2704,0.5244,0.3312]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q09.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 9","ov":[[0.1719,0.9244,0.3489,0.992]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s110~splenic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Splenic artery","B. Left gastric artery","C. Pancreatic artery","D. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","E. Short gastric arteries"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Splenic artery.</b> The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its largest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Spleen; also pancreatic, short gastric, left gastro-omental and posterior gastric branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then follow its left branch along the top of the pancreas toward the spleen.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Left gastric artery: The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, climbing to the esophagogastric junction and then running down the lesser curvature inside the lesser omentum. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its smallest branch).</b><br>C. Pancreatic artery: On a celiac angiogram the pancreatic arteries are the small branches dropping from the splenic artery into the pancreatic body and tail below it. <b>Comes from splenic artery, along the upper border of the pancreas.</b><br>D. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum.</b><br>E. Short gastric arteries: The short gastric arteries are four or five small branches of the splenic artery near the splenic hilum that run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus of the stomach. <b>Comes from splenic artery near the hilum, or its terminal splenic branches.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s110.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 110"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9428,0.3469,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.805}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":21,"label":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa and stomach bed","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8292,0.8593,0.995,0.8915]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st34~left-gastro-omental-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pancreatic artery","B. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery, near the splenic hilum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; anastomoses with the right gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; lesser sac behind</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic artery at the hilum of the spleen, then follow the branch that turns onto the greater curvature heading toward the pylorus.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Pancreatic artery: On a celiac angiogram the pancreatic arteries are the small branches dropping from the splenic artery into the pancreatic body and tail below it. <b>Comes from splenic artery, along the upper border of the pancreas.</b><br>C. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>D. Splenic artery: The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch).</b><br>E. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st34.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 34"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.491,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":21,"label":"Splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_296_1694.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.61","tb":1,"ov":[[0.3944,0.2704,0.5244,0.3312]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s110.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 110","ov":[[0.1719,0.9408,0.3489,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q09~splenic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","C. Pancreatic artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Splenic artery.</b> The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its largest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Spleen; also pancreatic, short gastric, left gastro-omental and posterior gastric branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then follow its left branch along the top of the pancreas toward the spleen.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>B. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus.</b><br>C. Pancreatic artery: On a celiac angiogram the pancreatic arteries are the small branches dropping from the splenic artery into the pancreatic body and tail below it. <b>Comes from splenic artery, along the upper border of the pancreas.</b><br>E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q09.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 9"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9264,0.3469,0.99,"",1]],"ar":0.615,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s110~splenic-artery"}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":22,"label":"Common hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6895,0.2981,0.9198,0.3411]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-q08.jpg","cap":"Common hepatic artery on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 8","ov":[[0.1719,0.9273,0.4389,0.9926]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s108~common-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Left gastric artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","E. Splenic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Common hepatic artery.</b> The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Divides into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries above the first part of duodenum</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, take its right-going branch, and stop at the point where it splits near the first part of the duodenum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Left gastric artery: The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, climbing to the esophagogastric junction and then running down the lesser curvature inside the lesser omentum. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its smallest branch).</b><br>C. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>D. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus.</b><br>E. Splenic artery: The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s108.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 108"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.4369,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.8}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":22,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg","cap":"Abdominal esophagus","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7679,0.8609,0.9914,0.9026]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s033~gastroduodenal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Left gastric artery","D. Gastroduodenal artery","E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus.</b><br>B. Common hepatic artery: The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries. <b>Comes from celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Left gastric artery: The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, climbing to the esophagogastric junction and then running down the lesser curvature inside the lesser omentum. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its smallest branch).</b><br>E. Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: The anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the gastroduodenal artery, runs down the front of the pancreatic head beside the duodenum to meet the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch, with the right gastro-omental).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The gastroduodenal artery branches off the common hepatic artery and runs behind the first part of the duodenum."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s033.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 33"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9473,0.4269,0.9942,"",1]],"ar":0.88}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":22,"label":"Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0448,0.4889,0.2714,0.5302]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q05~right-gastro-omental-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","C. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Gastroduodenal artery","E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery.</b> The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; joins the left gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery emerging below the first part of the duodenum, then follow its branch to the left along the greater curvature.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Common hepatic artery: The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries. <b>Comes from celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: The posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches from the gastroduodenal artery behind the duodenum and descends on the back of the pancreatic head beside the bile duct. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery, behind the first part of the duodenum.</b><br>D. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>E. Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The left gastro-omental artery is a branch of the splenic artery that runs along the greater curvature of the stomach inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q05.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 5"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9222,0.4783,0.9891,"",1]],"ar":0.58}]},{"l":"CAD-A2","u":22,"label":"Common hepatic artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg","cap":"Distribution of the common hepatic artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6895,0.2981,0.9198,0.3411]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s108.jpg","cap":"Common hepatic artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 108","ov":[[0.1719,0.9405,0.4389,0.9952]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q08~common-hepatic-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B. Gastroduodenal artery","C. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Common hepatic artery.</b> The common hepatic artery runs right from the celiac trunk along the pancreas's upper border, splitting near the duodenum's first part into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Divides into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries above the first part of duodenum</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, take its right-going branch, and stop at the point where it splits near the first part of the duodenum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: On these celiac and hepatic arteriograms the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries run down from the gastroduodenal artery in the curve between the duodenum and pancreatic head. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (anterior and posterior branches).</b><br>B. Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery leaves the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, splitting into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <b>Comes from common hepatic artery.</b><br>C. Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: The right gastro-omental artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs left along the greater curvature inside the greater omentum. <b>Comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus.</b><br>D. Splenic artery: The splenic artery is the largest celiac trunk branch, winding left along the upper border of the pancreas behind the stomach to reach the splenic hilum through the splenorenal ligament. <b>Comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q08.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 8"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9293,0.4369,0.9906,"",1]],"ar":0.64214,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s108~common-hepatic-artery"}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":23,"label":"Right lobe of liver","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0325,0.0825,0.125,0.154]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~right-lobe-of-liver","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Right lobe of liver","B. Left lobe of liver","C. Bare area of liver","D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right lobe of liver","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right lobe of liver.</b> The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament on the front of the liver, then the gallbladder and IVC on the underside; liver to the right of these is the right lobe.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Left lobe of liver: The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<br>C. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>E. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":23,"label":"Left lobe of liver","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8061,0.3034,0.9776,0.3755]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~left-lobe-of-liver","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Bare area of liver","B. Left lobe of liver","C. Right lobe of liver","D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left lobe of liver","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left lobe of liver.</b> The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament and the round ligament in its free edge, then follow them to the fissure on the underside; liver to the left is the left lobe.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>C. Right lobe of liver: The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<br>D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>E. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":24,"label":"Falciform ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg","cap":"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0157,0.1555,0.1615,0.2354]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st33.jpg","cap":"Falciform ligament on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 33","ov":[[0.1795,0.9374,0.4135,0.9941]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~falciform-ligament","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Coronary ligament (of liver)","D. Falciform ligament","E. Bare area of liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Falciform ligament.</b> The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Gallbladder: The gallbladder is the pear-shaped sac on the liver's underside between the right and quadrate lobes, resting on the transverse colon and first part of the duodenum.<br>B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>C. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>E. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":24,"label":"Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg","cap":"Parts of greater and lesser omenta","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0047,0.108,0.1391,0.1494]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~round-ligament-of-liver","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Coronary ligament (of liver)","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Left lobe of liver","D. Falciform ligament","E. Bare area of liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis).</b> The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Obliterated left umbilical vein</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Umbilicus to the left branch of the portal vein, via the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament between the anterior abdominal wall and the liver, then feel along its free lower edge for the firm cord running to the underside of the liver.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>C. Left lobe of liver: The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<br>D. Falciform ligament: The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<br>E. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":24,"label":"Coronary ligament (of liver)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1703.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7389,0.0805,0.9508,0.1805]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~coronary-ligament","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Coronary ligament (of liver)","B. Bare area of liver","C. Falciform ligament","D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","E. Right lobe of liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Coronary ligament (of liver)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Coronary ligament (of liver).</b> The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; peritoneum reflecting from the diaphragm onto the liver</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Diaphragm to the superior and posterior surface of the liver, enclosing the bare area</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Run a hand over the top of the right lobe toward the back until it is stopped by the peritoneal reflection to the diaphragm; that reflection is the coronary ligament.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>C. Falciform ligament: The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<br>D. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>E. Right lobe of liver: The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":24,"label":"Bare area of liver","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1702.jpg","cap":"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64","tb":1,"ov":[[0.215,0.2322,0.3522,0.3389]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~bare-area-of-liver","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","B. Bare area of liver","C. Coronary ligament (of liver)","D. Falciform ligament","E. Left lobe of liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bare area of liver","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bare area of liver.</b> The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Diaphragm directly, with no peritoneum; IVC (inferior vena cava) and right suprarenal medially</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC groove on the back of the liver, then the dull, non-shiny area to its right bounded by the coronary ligament layers.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>C. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>D. Falciform ligament: The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<br>E. Left lobe of liver: The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":24,"label":"Falciform ligament","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg","cap":"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0157,0.1555,0.1615,0.2354]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","cap":"Falciform ligament on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31","ov":[[0.005,0.3743,0.1977,0.4302]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st33~falciform-ligament","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","C. Left triangular ligament","D. Coronary ligament (of liver)","E. Bare area of liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Falciform ligament.</b> The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): The round ligament of the liver, the remnant of the left umbilical vein, runs from the umbilicus in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament to the liver.<br>C. Left triangular ligament: The left triangular ligament is a thin peritoneal fold tying the left tip of the left liver lobe to the diaphragm, where the coronary ligament's layers meet.<br>D. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>E. Bare area of liver: The bare area is the peritoneum-free back of the right liver lobe, lying on the diaphragm inside the coronary ligament layers, with the inferior vena cava groove medially.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st33.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 33"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.4115,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s031~falciform-ligament"}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":25,"label":"Gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg","cap":"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0047,0.5474,0.0993,0.5929]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s031~gallbladder","s":"What does the ? label point to?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Right lobe of liver","C. Left lobe of liver","D. Gallbladder","E. Coronary ligament (of liver)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gallbladder.</b> The gallbladder is the pear-shaped sac on the liver's underside between the right and quadrate lobes, resting on the transverse colon and first part of the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>First and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe at the right 9th costal cartilage in the midclavicular line, then lift the liver; the sac on its underside is the gallbladder.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Falciform ligament: The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<br>B. Right lobe of liver: The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<br>C. Left lobe of liver: The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<br>E. Coronary ligament (of liver): The coronary ligament is the peritoneal fold from the diaphragm onto the top and back of the liver, its two layers enclosing the bare area.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s031.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 31"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.007,0.3763,0.1957,0.4282,"",0],[0.007,0.7441,0.1957,0.7959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0441,0.993,0.0959,"",0],[0.8535,0.0963,0.993,0.1482,"",0],[0.007,0.3241,0.1957,0.3759,"",0],[0.8535,0.3041,0.993,0.3559,"",0],[0.007,0.9091,0.1957,0.9609,"",1]],"ar":0.46211}]},{"l":"CAD-A3","u":26,"label":"Pancreas","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply to the pancreas","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7515,0.7037,0.843,0.745]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st35~pancreas","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Anterior border of the spleen","B. Pancreas","C. Right lobe of liver","D. Falciform ligament","E. Left lobe of liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pancreas.</b> The pancreas lies behind the peritoneum and stomach across L1 to L2, from the duodenal C-loop on the right to the splenic hilum, with the splenic vein behind it.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left kidney, left suprarenal gland, aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava), behind the peritoneum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Lift the stomach or open the lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament, then look at the posterior wall: the gland lying across the aorta is the pancreas.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Anterior border of the spleen: In this axial section the anterior (superior) border of the spleen is its sharp, often notched front edge, between the diaphragmatic surface and the gastric impression, pointing toward the stomach.<br>C. Right lobe of liver: The right lobe is the liver lying right of the falciform ligament and, underneath, right of the gallbladder and inferior vena cava, above the right kidney and colic flexure.<br>D. Falciform ligament: The falciform ligament is the peritoneal fold tying the front of the liver to the abdominal wall above the umbilicus, carrying the round ligament in its free edge.<br>E. Left lobe of liver: The left lobe of the liver is the thinner part left of the falciform ligament, reaching into the left upper abdomen in front of the stomach.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st35.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 35"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.3636,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":27,"label":"Jejunum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8287,0.4081,0.919,0.4528]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st36~jejunum","s":"What is A?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Cecum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> The jejunum is the first two-fifths of the small intestine, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 and coiling mostly in the upper left abdomen.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 under the transverse mesocolon, then follow the loops away from it; hold the mesentery to the light to see the long vasa recta.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior mesenteric artery: The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk.</b><br>B. Cecum: The cecum is the blind-ended pouch of large bowel in the right iliac fossa, hanging below the ileocecal junction, with the appendix attached to its posteromedial side.<br>D. Ileum: The ileum is the last three-fifths of the small bowel past the duodenum, coiled in the lower right abdomen and pelvis, ending at the cecum's medial side.<br>E. Superior rectal artery: The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["A shows long vasa recta and few arcades (jejunum); B shows several tiers of arcades with short vasa recta (ileum)."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st36.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 36"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2722,0.9394,0.4543,0.9921,"",0],[0.0437,0.9394,0.2257,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":27,"label":"Ileum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg","cap":"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0993,0.7928,0.1726,0.8347]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st36~ileum","s":"What is B?","o":["A. Superior rectal artery","B. Cecum","C. Superior mesenteric vein","D. Jejunum","E. Ileum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ileum.</b> The ileum is the last three-fifths of the small bowel past the duodenum, coiled in the lower right abdomen and pelvis, ending at the cecum's medial side.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow backward the small intestine that enters its medial side; those terminal loops are ileum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior rectal artery: The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation.</b><br>B. Cecum: The cecum is the blind-ended pouch of large bowel in the right iliac fossa, hanging below the ileocecal junction, with the appendix attached to its posteromedial side.<br>C. Superior mesenteric vein: The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. <b>Drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein.</b><br>D. Jejunum: The jejunum is the first two-fifths of the small intestine, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 and coiling mostly in the upper left abdomen.<br>","ex":"","fl":["A shows long vasa recta and few arcades (jejunum); B shows several tiers of arcades with short vasa recta (ileum)."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st36.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 36"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.0437,0.9394,0.2257,0.9921,"",0],[0.2722,0.9394,0.4543,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":28,"label":"Cecum","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_347_366.jpg","cap":"Cecum and appendix","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.77","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0279,0.6632,0.1555,0.7128]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q20~cecum","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Ileum","D. Vermiform appendix","E. Cecum"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cecum","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cecum.</b> The cecum is the blind-ended pouch of large bowel in the right iliac fossa, hanging below the ileocecal junction, with the appendix attached to its posteromedial side.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the terminal ileum and the ileocecal junction; the large sac hanging below that junction is the cecum, and its three teniae meet at the appendix.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior mesenteric vein: The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. <b>Drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein.</b><br>B. Superior mesenteric artery: The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Ileum: The ileum is the last three-fifths of the small bowel past the duodenum, coiled in the lower right abdomen and pelvis, ending at the cecum's medial side.<br>D. Vermiform appendix: The appendix is a narrow blind tube arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum below the ileocecal junction, where the three teniae coli meet, hung on the mesoappendix.<br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q20.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 20"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.3469,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.8}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":29,"label":"Superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116","tb":1,"ov":[[0.525,0.1147,0.8671,0.156]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s041.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 41","ov":[[0.1719,0.9406,0.4731,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st37~superior-mesenteric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries","B. Superior rectal artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior mesenteric artery.</b> The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Midgut: duodenum below the major papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum, then the artery emerging between them just left of the superior mesenteric vein; trace it up to the aorta.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries: On a superior mesenteric angiogram the jejunal and ileal branches are the many arteries fanning from the left side of the superior mesenteric artery toward the small bowel. <b>Comes from left side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), about 15–18 branches.</b><br>B. Superior rectal artery: The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation.</b><br>D. Superior mesenteric vein: The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. <b>Drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein.</b><br>E. Inferior mesenteric artery: The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L3, behind the third part of the duodenum, and runs down and left behind the peritoneum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st37.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 37"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1815,0.9394,0.493,0.9921,"",1]],"ar":0.80857}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":29,"label":"Superior mesenteric vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg","cap":"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41","tb":1,"ov":[[0.119,0.8825,0.3394,0.9341]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s116~superior-mesenteric-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","D. Jejunum","E. Inferior mesenteric vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Superior mesenteric vein.</b> The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas at its end; the root of the mesentery below that</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery below the pancreas, then take the wider, thin-walled vessel immediately to its right and follow it up behind the pancreatic neck.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Superior mesenteric artery: The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>D. Jejunum: The jejunum is the first two-fifths of the small intestine, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 and coiling mostly in the upper left abdomen.<br>E. Inferior mesenteric vein: The inferior mesenteric vein climbs the left posterior abdominal wall, just left of the duodenojejunal flexure, then passes behind the pancreas to usually drain into the splenic vein. <b>Drains into splenic vein behind the pancreas (usual); sometimes the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) or their junction.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s116.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 116"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9424,0.4533,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.79857}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":29,"label":"Superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116","tb":1,"ov":[[0.525,0.1147,0.8671,0.156]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st37.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric artery on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 37","ov":[[0.1795,0.9374,0.495,0.9941]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s041~superior-mesenteric-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Superior mesenteric vein","D. Superior rectal artery","E. Inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Superior mesenteric artery.</b> The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Midgut: duodenum below the major papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum, then the artery emerging between them just left of the superior mesenteric vein; trace it up to the aorta.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery: The posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery branches from the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery off the superior mesenteric artery and climbs behind the pancreatic head beside the duodenum. <b>Comes from SMA (superior mesenteric artery), usually its first branch, or the first jejunal artery.</b><br>C. Superior mesenteric vein: The superior mesenteric vein climbs just right of the superior mesenteric artery and joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. <b>Drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein.</b><br>D. Superior rectal artery: The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation.</b><br>E. Inferior mesenteric artery: The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L3, behind the third part of the duodenum, and runs down and left behind the peritoneum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s041.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 41"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9426,0.4711,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.80214,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st37~superior-mesenteric-artery"}]},{"l":"CAD-A4","u":30,"label":"Superior rectal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_400_1903.jpg","cap":"Arteries and veins of rectum and anal canal","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.32","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0131,0.0184,0.2905,0.0724]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-q07~superior-rectal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Superior rectal artery","C. Left colic artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Sigmoid arteries"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior rectal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Superior rectal artery.</b> The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, crossing the left common iliac vessels in the sigmoid mesocolon root to reach the back of the rectum.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectum via two branches beside it; anastomoses with the middle rectal arteries</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery, then follow its main stem downward past the sigmoid branches over the pelvic brim to the back of the rectum.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Superior mesenteric artery: The superior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L1, runs down behind the pancreatic neck, then crosses over the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk.</b><br>C. Left colic artery: The left colic artery is the first branch of the inferior mesenteric artery, running left behind the peritoneum toward the descending colon. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its first branch.</b><br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery: The inferior mesenteric artery leaves the front of the aorta at L3, behind the third part of the duodenum, and runs down and left behind the peritoneum. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L3, 3–4 cm above the bifurcation.</b><br>E. Sigmoid arteries: The sigmoid arteries are two to four branches of the inferior mesenteric artery that run down and left in the sigmoid mesocolon, between the left colic and superior rectal arteries. <b>Comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), usually 2–4 branches.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-q07.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 7"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9359,0.4147,0.9919,"",1]],"ar":0.71214}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":31,"label":"Ureter","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.809,0.7503,0.8727,0.7894]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s039.jpg","cap":"Ureter on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 39","ov":[[0.1719,0.9405,0.3489,0.9952]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st40~ureter","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","B. Ureter","C. Left renal vein","D. Renal pyramid (medulla)","E. Renal pelvis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ureter","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ureter.</b> Each ureter runs from the renal pelvis down the front of psoas major behind the peritoneum, crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels, and enters the pelvis over the common iliac bifurcation.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, at the ureteropelvic junction</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; mesentery root (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow the tube down on psoas to the pelvic brim; check that the gonadal vessels cross in front of it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>C. Left renal vein: The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. <b>Drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>D. Renal pyramid (medulla): The renal pyramids make up the medulla: striped cones deep to the cortex, separated by renal columns, with their tips (papillae) pointing into the minor calyces.<br>E. Renal pelvis: The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped top of the ureter, formed in the renal sinus by the major calyces and lying behind the renal vessels at the hilum. <b>Empties into ureter, at the ureteropelvic junction near the hilum.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st40.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 40"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2515,0.9508,0.4998,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.39616}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":31,"label":"Ureter","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.809,0.7503,0.8727,0.7894]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st40.jpg","cap":"Ureter on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 40","ov":[[0.2495,0.9488,0.5018,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s039~ureter","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Ureter","B. Renal pelvis","C. Renal artery","D. Renal pyramid (medulla)","E. Minor calyx"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ureter","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ureter.</b> Each ureter runs from the renal pelvis down the front of psoas major behind the peritoneum, crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels, and enters the pelvis over the common iliac bifurcation.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, at the ureteropelvic junction</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; mesentery root (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow the tube down on psoas to the pelvic brim; check that the gonadal vessels cross in front of it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Renal pelvis: The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped top of the ureter, formed in the renal sinus by the major calyces and lying behind the renal vessels at the hilum. <b>Empties into ureter, at the ureteropelvic junction near the hilum.</b><br>C. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>D. Renal pyramid (medulla): The renal pyramids make up the medulla: striped cones deep to the cortex, separated by renal columns, with their tips (papillae) pointing into the minor calyces.<br>E. Minor calyx: Each minor calyx is a small cup in the renal sinus wrapped around the tip (papilla) of a renal pyramid, joining others to form a major calyx. <b>Empties into major calyx (several minor calyces join each one).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s039.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 39"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.3469,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.8,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st40~ureter"}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Renal artery","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_325_1756.jpg","cap":"Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.84","tb":1,"ov":[[0.0178,0.5402,0.1994,0.6218]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s112~renal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left renal vein","B. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","C. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","D. Renal artery","E. Segmental arteries of kidney"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Renal artery.</b> Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Kidney via five segmental arteries; also the inferior suprarenal artery and upper ureter</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Crus of the diaphragm and psoas major; renal pelvis behind it at the hilum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Renal vein; the right also passes behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta just below the SMA origin and follow the lateral branch to the kidney; at the hilum check that the vein is in front of it and the pelvis behind it.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left renal vein: The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. <b>Drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>B. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>C. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian): The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2).</b><br>E. Segmental arteries of kidney: The segmental arteries are the five end-arteries the renal artery splits into at the renal hilum, each feeding one kidney segment before dividing into interlobar arteries in the renal sinus. <b>Comes from renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s112.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 112"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9428,0.3469,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.805}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Left renal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_405_445.jpg","cap":"Renal vasculature","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.142","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6776,0.8973,0.7989,0.943]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s035.jpg","cap":"Left renal vein on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 35","ov":[[0.1719,0.941,0.3489,0.9953]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st38~left-renal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","B. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","C. Renal artery","D. Left renal vein","E. Right renal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left renal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left renal vein.</b> The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left kidney, plus the left suprarenal and left gonadal veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal artery, then the abdominal aorta, which it crosses in front of</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery) at the midline; pancreas and splenic vein laterally</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMA origin on the front of the aorta; the vein running horizontally between the SMA (in front) and the aorta (behind) is the left renal vein. Trace it left to the kidney and right into the IVC.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian): The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2).</b><br>B. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>C. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>E. Right renal vein: In axial sections the right renal vein is the short, wide vein running straight from the right renal hilum, in front of the renal artery, into the inferior vena cava. <b>Drains into right side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st38.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 38"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2515,0.9508,0.5046,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.39616}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_344_1790.jpg","cap":"Inferior vena cava and its tributaries","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.99","tb":1,"ov":[[0.7018,0.4467,0.9151,0.5298]]}],"q":[{"id":"pr_st39~left-gonadal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal artery","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","D. Left renal vein","E. Right renal vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein.</b> The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Pampiniform plexus (testis) or ovarian venous plexus (ovary)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left renal vein, entering at a right angle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left psoas major and the left ureter, which it crosses in front of</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed in front by the left colic vessels and the descending colon</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the left renal vein crossing the aorta, then follow the vein entering its lower border down along psoas.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian): The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2).</b><br>D. Left renal vein: The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. <b>Drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>E. Right renal vein: In axial sections the right renal vein is the short, wide vein running straight from the right renal hilum, in front of the renal artery, into the inferior vena cava. <b>Drains into right side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/pr_st39.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2026 practice practical","n":"Practice practical, station 39"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.2515,0.9508,0.5442,0.9945,"",1]],"ar":1.39616}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8406,0.6989,0.9704,0.7608]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S06-s023.jpg","cap":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian) on another picture","where":"2020 TA mock practical, question 23","ov":[[0.1719,0.9266,0.3917,0.9924]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s023~gonadal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Renal artery","B. Left renal vein","C. Segmental arteries of kidney","D. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","E. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian).</b> The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Testis via the spermatic cord, or ovary via the suspensory ligament of the ovary</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major and the ureter; the right one also crosses in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed by the duodenum and colic vessels (right) or left colic vessels (left)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter on psoas; the thin vessels crossing in front of it obliquely are the gonadal vessels. Follow the narrower one up to its origin on the aorta just below the renal arteries.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>B. Left renal vein: The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. <b>Drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>C. Segmental arteries of kidney: The segmental arteries are the five end-arteries the renal artery splits into at the renal hilum, each feeding one kidney segment before dividing into interlobar arteries in the renal sinus. <b>Comes from renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions.</b><br>E. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s023.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 23"}],"tier":1,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9425,0.3897,0.9932,"",1]],"ar":0.8}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Left renal vein","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_gray_405_445.jpg","cap":"Renal vasculature","where":"Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.142","tb":1,"ov":[[0.6776,0.8973,0.7989,0.943]]},{"src":"../img/pr_st38.jpg","cap":"Left renal vein on another picture","where":"Practice practical, station 38","ov":[[0.2495,0.9488,0.5066,0.9965]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S05-s035~left-renal-vein","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","C. Left renal vein","D. Renal artery","E. Right renal vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left renal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Left renal vein.</b> The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left kidney, plus the left suprarenal and left gonadal veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal artery, then the abdominal aorta, which it crosses in front of</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery) at the midline; pancreas and splenic vein laterally</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMA origin on the front of the aorta; the vein running horizontally between the SMA (in front) and the aorta (behind) is the left renal vein. Trace it left to the kidney and right into the IVC.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>A. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>B. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian): The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava. <b>Comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2).</b><br>D. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>E. Right renal vein: In axial sections the right renal vein is the short, wide vein running straight from the right renal hilum, in front of the renal artery, into the inferior vena cava. <b>Drains into right side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S05-s035.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2021 TA mock practical","n":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 35"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.943,0.3469,0.9933,"",1]],"ar":0.80857,"kind":"repeat","like":"pr_st38~left-renal-vein"}]},{"l":"CAD-A5","u":32,"label":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","slides":[{"src":"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg","cap":"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands","where":"Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76","tb":1,"ov":[[0.8406,0.6989,0.9704,0.7608]]},{"src":"../img/ta_S05-s023.jpg","cap":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian) on another picture","where":"2021 TA mock practical, slide 23","ov":[[0.1719,0.9405,0.3917,0.9952]]}],"q":[{"id":"ta_S06-s023~gonadal-artery","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","B. Renal artery","C. Left renal vein","D. Segmental arteries of kidney","E. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian).</b> The gonadal arteries leave the aorta just below the renal arteries and run down on psoas over the ureter; the right one also crosses the inferior vena cava.<dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Testis via the spermatic cord, or ovary via the suspensory ligament of the ovary</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major and the ureter; the right one also crosses in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed by the duodenum and colic vessels (right) or left colic vessels (left)</dd></dl><i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter on psoas; the thin vessels crossing in front of it obliquely are the gonadal vessels. Follow the narrower one up to its origin on the aorta just below the renal arteries.<br><span class=\"wl\">Why not the others</span>B. Renal artery: Each renal artery leaves the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right runs behind the inferior vena cava, the left behind the left renal vein. <b>Comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b><br>C. Left renal vein: The left renal vein crosses in front of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to reach the inferior vena cava, receiving the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. <b>Drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava).</b><br>D. Segmental arteries of kidney: The segmental arteries are the five end-arteries the renal artery splits into at the renal hilum, each feeding one kidney segment before dividing into interlobar arteries in the renal sinus. <b>Comes from renal artery, at or near the hilum, via its anterior and posterior divisions.</b><br>E. Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein: The left gonadal vein climbs on the left psoas major in front of the ureter and drains at a right angle into the left renal vein. <b>Drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle.</b><br>","ex":"","fl":["The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein; the ureter is posterior to both."],"ch":false,"img":"../img/ta_S06-s023.jpg","in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA mock practical","n":"2020 TA mock practical, question 23"}],"tier":2,"ob":[[0.1739,0.9286,0.3897,0.9904,"",1]],"ar":0.635,"kind":"repeat","like":"ta_S05-s023~gonadal-artery"}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"CAD-T1","h":"Internal thoracic artery and transversus thoracis","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Internal thoracic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>First part of the subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior intercostal arteries of spaces 1-6; ends as musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and pleura above; transversus thoracis separates it from pleura lower down</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</dd></dl><p>The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and descends behind the costal cartilages about 1 cm lateral to the edge of the sternum, anterior to transversus thoracis and the parietal pleura, accompanied by internal thoracic veins. It gives anterior intercostal arteries and in the 6th intercostal space divides into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn over the removed anterior chest wall and find the lateral edge of the sternum first; the vertical artery running parallel to it about a finger-width lateral, behind the cartilages, is the internal thoracic artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Transversus thoracis muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals</dd></dl><p>Transversus thoracis lies on the internal (posterior) surface of the anterior thoracic wall: its slips arise from the lower body of the sternum and xiphoid process and fan superolaterally to the inner surfaces of costal cartilages 2-6. The internal thoracic vessels run between it and the costal cartilages, so the muscle is deep (posterior) to them; it is continuous below with transversus abdominis. <i>Finding it:</i> Turn the removed anterior chest wall (sternum and cartilages) over and find the internal thoracic artery first; the thin muscle slips covering it and fanning out from the lower sternum are transversus thoracis.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.54%;top:27.14%;width:23.74%;height:4.89%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Internal thoracic artery: Arteries of thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_118_1347.jpg\" alt=\"Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.94%;top:59.33%;width:18.78%;height:4.60%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Transversus thoracis muscle: Dissection of anterior aspect of anterior thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.13</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Internal thoracic artery</b>: comes from first part of the subclavian artery; on top of endothoracic fascia and pleura above; under costal cartilages 1-6 and internal intercostals, about 1 cm lateral to the sternum</li><li><b>Transversus thoracis muscle</b>: arises from inner surface of the lower body of the sternum and the xiphoid process; on top of endothoracic fascia and parietal pleura; under internal thoracic vessels, then the costal cartilages and internal intercostals</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Internal thoracic artery","Transversus thoracis muscle"]},{"l":"CAD-T2","h":"Lungs, lobes and fissures","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior (upper) lobe of right lung</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Middle lobe below (horizontal fissure), inferior lobe behind; medially SVC (superior vena cava) and trachea</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Suprapleural membrane at its apex; upper ribs and pectoral muscles in front</dd></dl><p>The superior lobe of the right lung forms the apex and the upper anterior part of the right lung; it is bounded below anteriorly by the horizontal fissure and behind by the upper part of the oblique fissure. Its bronchus is the superior lobar (eparterial) bronchus, which arises above the right pulmonary artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the horizontal fissure first; the lobe above it (and in front of the upper oblique fissure) is the right superior lobe.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Horizontal fissure (right lung)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Middle lobe below it; meets the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</dd></dl><p>The horizontal fissure is present in the right lung and absent from the left; it runs forward horizontally from the oblique fissure at about the midaxillary line along the level of the 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, separating the superior lobe from the middle lobe. It is often incomplete. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure on the lateral surface of the right lung first; the fissure that branches off it and runs forward horizontally is the horizontal fissure.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg\" alt=\"Costal surfaces of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:10.67%;top:22.39%;width:11.34%;height:4.39%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior (upper) lobe of right lung: Costal surfaces of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_141_1401.jpg\" alt=\"Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.24%;top:58.07%;width:13.64%;height:7.92%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Horizontal fissure (right lung): Mediastinal surfaces and hila of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.34</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Middle lobe of right lung</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right atrium (right heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the right 4th-6th costal cartilages</dd></dl><p>The middle lobe is the small wedge-shaped lobe of the right lung lying between the horizontal fissure above and the oblique fissure below and behind; it lies anteriorly and laterally against the right side of the heart and does not reach the posterior surface. It is the right-side counterpart of the lingula. <i>Finding it:</i> Find both fissures of the right lung first; the lobe enclosed between the horizontal fissure and the lower anterior oblique fissure is the middle lobe.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Oblique fissure</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front; runs from the T2 spine to the 6th costal cartilage</dd></dl><p>The oblique fissure is present in both lungs; it runs from the posterior surface at about the level of the T2 spinous process downward and forward to about the 6th costal cartilage anteriorly, usually cutting completely through the lung to the hilum. It separates the inferior lobe (behind and below) from the superior lobe on the left and from the superior and middle lobes on the right. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the long cleft that starts high on the back of the lung and reaches the inferior border anteriorly; on the right confirm the horizontal fissure branches from it.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right diaphragm dome over the liver; esophagus and IVC (inferior vena cava) medially</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior and middle lobes in front, across the oblique fissure</dd></dl><p>The inferior lobe of the right lung lies posterior and inferior to the oblique fissure; it forms most of the posterior surface and the diaphragmatic surface of the right lung. On the mediastinal surface the esophageal impression and pulmonary ligament lie on it behind the hilum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the oblique fissure first; the large lobe behind and below it is the inferior lobe (on the right, confirm a horizontal fissure exists in front).</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior (upper) lobe of right lung</b>: on top of middle lobe below (horizontal fissure), inferior lobe behind; under suprapleural membrane at its apex</li><li><b>Horizontal fissure (right lung)</b>: on top of middle lobe below it; under right 4th rib and 4th costal cartilage, which mark its course</li><li><b>Middle lobe of right lung</b>: on top of right atrium (right heart border) medially; under anterior chest wall at the right 4th-6th costal cartilages</li><li><b>Oblique fissure</b>: on top of inferior lobe, which lies behind and below it; under superior lobe (and right middle lobe) in front</li><li><b>Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung</b>: on top of right diaphragm dome over the liver; under superior and middle lobes in front, across the oblique fissure</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Superior (upper) lobe of right lung","Horizontal fissure (right lung)","Middle lobe of right lung","Oblique fissure","Inferior (lower) lobe of right lung"]},{"l":"CAD-T2","h":"Lung borders, cardiac notch and lingula","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Lingula (of left lung)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left ventricle (left heart border) medially; inferior lobe behind across the oblique fissure</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages</dd></dl><p>The lingula is the thin, tongue-shaped inferior projection of the superior lobe of the left lung, below the cardiac notch, lying over the left side of the heart. It is part of the superior lobe (no fissure separates it) and corresponds to the middle lobe of the right lung. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cardiac notch on the anterior border of the left lung first; the tongue of lung tissue curling below and in front of it is the lingula.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_138_1395.jpg\" alt=\"Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.05%;top:67.80%;width:17.64%;height:7.34%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Lingula (of left lung): Relationship of thoracic contents and linings of thoracic cage <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.31</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Lingula (of left lung)</b>: on top of left ventricle (left heart border) medially; under anterior chest wall at the left 5th-6th costal cartilages</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Lingula (of left lung)"]},{"l":"CAD-T2","h":"Larynx to the main bronchi","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right main (primary) bronchus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</dd></dl><p>The right main bronchus is shorter (about 2.5 cm), wider and more vertical than the left; it gives off the superior lobar bronchus before entering the hilum and continues as the bronchus intermedius. The arch of the azygos vein passes over it, and at the hilum it lies posterior to the right pulmonary artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina at the tracheal bifurcation first; the short, steep, wide bronchus going right, under the azygos arch, is the right main bronchus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left main (primary) bronchus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Esophagus and descending aorta behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Aortic arch hooking over it; left pulmonary artery in front, then above</dd></dl><p>The left main bronchus is longer (about 5 cm), narrower and more horizontal than the right; it passes inferior to the aortic arch and anterior to the esophagus and descending aorta to reach the left hilum, where it lies below and behind the left pulmonary artery. It divides into superior and inferior lobar bronchi. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the carina first; the long bronchus running left under the aortic arch, below the left pulmonary artery, is the left main bronchus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_194_1507.jpg\" alt=\"Deep dissection of superior mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.71%;top:57.71%;width:9.25%;height:4.21%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right main (primary) bronchus: Deep dissection of superior mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.69</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right main (primary) bronchus</b>: on top of right vagus nerve and azygos vein behind, the azygos then arching over it; under right pulmonary artery, then the SVC (superior vena cava) and ascending aorta in front</li><li><b>Left main (primary) bronchus</b>: on top of esophagus and descending aorta behind it; under aortic arch hooking over it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Right main (primary) bronchus","Left main (primary) bronchus"]},{"l":"CAD-T2","h":"Lung root at the hilum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right pulmonary artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it</dd></dl><p>The right pulmonary artery leaves the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk in the concavity of the aortic arch and runs horizontally to the right, posterior to the ascending aorta and SVC and anterior to the right main bronchus, to reach the right hilum. It is longer than the left and at the hilum lies anterior to the bronchus and below the superior lobar (eparterial) bronchus. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk first and follow its right branch; the vessel that disappears behind the ascending aorta and SVC toward the right hilum is the right pulmonary artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left pulmonary artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; shorter than the right</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left lung lobes, via lobar and segmental arteries beside the bronchi</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Descending aorta behind; it arches over the left main bronchus</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left phrenic nerve in front; aortic arch above, tied to it by the ligamentum arteriosum</dd></dl><p>The left pulmonary artery is the shorter branch of the pulmonary trunk; it passes to the left in front of the descending aorta and arches over the left main bronchus to become the most superior structure in the left hilum. The ligamentum arteriosum connects its origin to the underside of the aortic arch. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum under the aortic arch first; the pulmonary branch it attaches to, heading left over the left bronchus, is the left pulmonary artery.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_145_1414.jpg\" alt=\"Pulmonary circulation\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.51%;top:12.16%;width:14.36%;height:7.77%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right pulmonary artery: Pulmonary circulation <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.37</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_196_1509.jpg\" alt=\"(Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:5.27%;top:59.25%;width:16.59%;height:4.00%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left pulmonary artery: (Continued) B. The left side of the mediastinum, the “red side,” is dominated by arterial structures: the arch of aorta and thoracic aorta, left common carotid <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pulmonary veins</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Each lung, as superior and inferior veins per side; right middle lobe vein joins the superior</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Main bronchus behind them in the root; descending aorta behind the left veins</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Phrenic nerve in front; right veins pass behind the SVC (superior vena cava) and right atrium</dd></dl><p>There are usually two pulmonary veins on each side (superior and inferior), forming the most anterior and most inferior structures of the root of the lung; they pass medially to open into the posterior wall of the left atrium. The inferior pulmonary vein lies at the lowest point of the hilum, just above the pulmonary ligament. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left atrium on the back of the heart (or the lowest part of the hilum) first; the vessels entering the left atrium from each side are the pulmonary veins.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right pulmonary artery</b>: comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation under the aortic arch; on top of carina and right main bronchus, directly behind it; under ascending aorta and SVC (superior vena cava), in front of it</li><li><b>Left pulmonary artery</b>: comes from pulmonary trunk, at its bifurcation; on top of descending aorta behind; under left phrenic nerve in front</li><li><b>Pulmonary veins</b>: drains into left atrium, posterior wall, usually by four separate openings; on top of main bronchus behind them in the root; under phrenic nerve in front</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Right pulmonary artery","Left pulmonary artery","Pulmonary veins"]},{"l":"CAD-T3","h":"Aortic arch and its branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Brachiocephalic trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right subclavian and right common carotid arteries, behind the right sternoclavicular joint</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front; then the manubrium and thymus remnant</dd></dl><p>Arises from the beginning of the arch behind the manubrium, ascends to the right, first anterior to and then on the right side of the trachea, passing posterior to the left brachiocephalic vein, and divides behind the right sternoclavicular joint into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries. It usually has no branches before it divides. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left brachiocephalic vein and lift it; the large artery directly behind it, running from the arch up toward the right sternoclavicular joint, is the brachiocephalic trunk.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ligamentum arteriosum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fetal ductus arteriosus, closed after birth</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Origin of the left pulmonary artery to the underside of the aortic arch</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Deep cardiac plexus and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks just behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the left vagus nerve crossing lateral to it</dd></dl><p>The fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus, running from the beginning of the left pulmonary artery (at the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk) up to the inferior concave surface of the aortic arch. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the arch immediately lateral to it. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and follow it up to its bifurcation under the arch; the solid cord connecting that point to the arch is the ligamentum arteriosum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_192_1503.jpg\" alt=\"Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:50.14%;top:13.21%;width:36.74%;height:5.54%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Brachiocephalic trunk: Common pattern of branches of arch of aorta <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.67</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_193_1504.jpg\" alt=\"Great vessels and nerves\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:68.62%;top:75.84%;width:14.91%;height:10.55%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ligamentum arteriosum: Great vessels and nerves <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.68</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Brachiocephalic trunk</b>: comes from arch of the aorta, its first and largest branch, behind the manubrium; on top of trachea, crossing in front of it to lie on its right side; under left brachiocephalic vein crossing in front</li><li><b>Ligamentum arteriosum</b>: on top of deep cardiac plexus and the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, which hooks just behind it; under mediastinal pleura, with the left vagus nerve crossing lateral to it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Brachiocephalic trunk","Ligamentum arteriosum"]},{"l":"CAD-T3","h":"Phrenic and vagus nerves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Phrenic nerve (right/left)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Diaphragm (its only motor supply); sensory to pericardium, mediastinal and diaphragmatic pleura</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; right over SVC (superior vena cava), left over left ventricle</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</dd></dl><p>Each phrenic nerve (C3–C5) descends between the fibrous pericardium and the mediastinal pleura, anterior to the root of the lung and accompanied by the pericardiacophrenic artery and vein, to the diaphragm. The right one runs along the right brachiocephalic vein, SVC, right atrium and IVC; the left one crosses the left side of the aortic arch anterior to the left vagus and descends over the left ventricle. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the root of the lung from its medial side; the nerve running vertically in front of the root on the pericardium is the phrenic, the one behind the root is the vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Vagus nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla oblongata (cranial nerve X), leaving the skull through the jugular foramen</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Larynx, heart, lungs and the gut to the left colic flexure (parasympathetic)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Trachea (right) or aortic arch (left), then the esophagus behind the lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Lung root in front of it; mediastinal pleura on its lateral side</dd></dl><p>The vagus nerve descends in the carotid sheath between the internal jugular vein and carotid artery, then passes behind the lung root to form the esophageal plexus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_140_1400.jpg\" alt=\"Costal surfaces of lungs\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:75.09%;top:22.87%;width:11.68%;height:4.39%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Phrenic nerve (right/left): Costal surfaces of lungs <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.33</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_179_1480.jpg\" alt=\"Cardiac nerves and plexus\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:82.46%;top:13.86%;width:12.46%;height:4.68%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Vagus nerve: Cardiac nerves and plexus <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.63</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right vagus nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); enters the thorax crossing in front of the right subclavian artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the posterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front; azygos arch lateral to it</dd></dl><p>Enters the thorax anterior to the right subclavian artery (where it gives the right recurrent laryngeal nerve), then descends on the right side of the trachea, posterior to the right brachiocephalic vein and SVC and medial to the arch of the azygos vein, passes posterior to the root of the right lung and spreads onto the esophagus as the esophageal plexus. Below the plexus its fibres mainly form the posterior vagal trunk. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the arch of the azygos vein; the nerve passing medial to the arch on the side of the trachea and then behind the right lung root is the right vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left vagus nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Medulla (cranial nerve X); descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Left recurrent laryngeal, cardiac and pulmonary branches, esophageal plexus, then mainly the anterior vagal trunk</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</dd></dl><p>Descends between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, crosses the left side of the aortic arch posterior to the left phrenic nerve, gives the left recurrent laryngeal nerve at the inferior border of the arch, then passes posterior to the root of the left lung onto the esophagus (esophageal plexus). Below the plexus its fibres mainly form the anterior vagal trunk. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left side of the aortic arch; of the two nerves crossing it, follow the posterior one: if it gives a branch that hooks under the arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, it is the left vagus.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid; laryngeal mucosa below the vocal folds</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum, then the left tracheoesophageal groove</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum at its loop; left thyroid lobe higher up</dd></dl><p>Arises from the left vagus at the inferior border of the aortic arch, hooks under the arch immediately lateral (and posterior) to the ligamentum arteriosum, and ascends to the larynx in the groove between the trachea and esophagus. Its loop lies at sternal-angle level, lower than the loop of the right recurrent laryngeal nerve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk bifurcation and the underside of the arch; the nerve curving under the arch just lateral to it is the left recurrent laryngeal.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Phrenic nerve (right/left)</b>: comes from ventral rami of C3, C4 and C5 (mainly C4); on top of fibrous pericardium in front of the lung root; under mediastinal pleura, with the pericardiacophrenic vessels running beside it</li><li><b>Vagus nerve</b>: comes from medulla oblongata (cranial nerve X), leaving the skull through the jugular foramen; on top of trachea (right) or aortic arch (left), then the esophagus behind the lung root; under lung root in front of it</li><li><b>Right vagus nerve</b>: comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); on top of right side of the trachea, then the esophagus behind the right lung root; under SVC (superior vena cava) and right lung root in front</li><li><b>Left vagus nerve</b>: comes from medulla (cranial nerve X); on top of left side of the aortic arch, then the esophagus behind the left lung root; under left superior intercostal vein on the arch, left phrenic nerve in front, then left lung root</li><li><b>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</b>: comes from left vagus nerve, at the lower border of the aortic arch; on top of under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum, then the left tracheoesophageal groove; under aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum at its loop</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Phrenic nerve (right/left)","Vagus nerve","Right vagus nerve","Left vagus nerve","Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"]},{"l":"CAD-T3","h":"Descending aorta and intercostal vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thoracic (descending) aorta</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Posterior intercostal (3rd–11th), subcostal, bronchial and esophageal arteries; becomes abdominal aorta at T12</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies; hemiazygos veins cross behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down</dd></dl><p>Continues the aortic arch from about T4, descends in the posterior mediastinum on the left side of the vertebral bodies, gradually moving toward the midline and lying posterior to the root of the left lung, the left atrium and (lower down) the esophagus, and leaves through the aortic hiatus at T12 as the abdominal aorta. It gives the 3rd–11th posterior intercostal and the subcostal arteries from its back, plus small bronchial, esophageal and pericardial branches. <i>Finding it:</i> Pull the left lung forward and look behind its root; the large artery on the left side of the vertebral column, with paired intercostal branches, is the thoracic aorta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior intercostal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); spaces 1–2 from the supreme intercostal (costocervical trunk)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Intercostal space and overlying skin; dorsal branch to back muscles and spinal cord</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Internal intercostal muscle, in the costal groove of the rib above</dd></dl><p>Posterior intercostal arteries of spaces 3-11 arise from the posterior aspect of the thoracic aorta (spaces 1-2 from the superior intercostal artery of the costocervical trunk) and run laterally in the costal groove between the posterior intercostal vein above and the intercostal nerve below. Because the aorta is left of the midline, the right ones are longer and cross the vertebral bodies posterior to the esophagus, thoracic duct and azygos vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the descending thoracic aorta first; the paired small branches leaving its back at each level and running into the intercostal spaces are the posterior intercostal arteries.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_199_1513.jpg\" alt=\"Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.79%;top:70.36%;width:14.79%;height:6.36%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Thoracic (descending) aorta: Thoracic duct and bronchomediastinal trunks <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.73</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_123_1359.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries of thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.54%;top:41.25%;width:16.62%;height:8.14%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Posterior intercostal artery: Arteries of thoracic wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.19</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Thoracic (descending) aorta</b>: comes from aortic arch, continuing at the T4–T5 level (sternal angle); on top of left side of the thoracic vertebral bodies; under left lung root, pericardium over the left atrium, then the esophagus in front lower down</li><li><b>Posterior intercostal artery</b>: comes from thoracic aorta (spaces 3–11); on top of innermost intercostal muscle, then endothoracic fascia and costal pleura; under internal intercostal muscle, in the costal groove of the rib above</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Thoracic (descending) aorta","Posterior intercostal artery"]},{"l":"CAD-T3","h":"Azygos system and thoracic duct","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Hemiazygos vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left ascending lumbar and left subcostal veins, plus the lowest left posterior intercostals</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct</dd></dl><p>Formed on the left from the left ascending lumbar and subcostal veins, it ascends on the left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies, receiving the lowest left posterior intercostal veins (about 9th–11th), and usually at about T9 crosses to the right, posterior to the aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct, to join the azygos vein. Its crossing level and its connection with the accessory hemiazygos vary. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein on the right; look for a vein joining it from the left across the front of the lower vertebral bodies behind the aorta, and follow it back to the left side.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Accessory hemiazygos vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces; sometimes left bronchial veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Descending thoracic aorta in front; it crosses behind the aorta and thoracic duct</dd></dl><p>Begins at about the fourth or fifth left intercostal space and descends on the left side of the vertebral bodies from about T5 to T8, receiving the left posterior intercostal veins of about the 4th–8th spaces (sometimes the left bronchial veins), then crosses posterior to the aorta and thoracic duct at about T7–T8 to the azygos vein. It may join the hemiazygos before crossing and may connect above with the left superior intercostal vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein, then look for veins joining it from the left: the higher crossing (about T7–T8), fed from the upper left intercostal spaces, is the accessory hemiazygos.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_200_1515.jpg\" alt=\"Azygos system of veins\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:60.63%;top:45.33%;width:25.91%;height:4.90%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Hemiazygos vein: Azygos system of veins <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.74</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_184_141.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of the thoracic wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.80%;top:21.47%;width:22.66%;height:5.19%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Accessory hemiazygos vein: Veins of the thoracic wall <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.30</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Thoracic duct</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Begins</dt><dd>Cisterna chyli at about L1–L2, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Esophagus in front; azygos vein on its right and descending aorta on its left</dd></dl><p>Begins at the cisterna chyli in the abdomen, enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus and ascends on the front of the vertebral bodies posterior to the esophagus, between the azygos vein (right) and descending aorta (left); at about T4–T6 it crosses to the left and ascends along the left side of the esophagus into the neck to end at the left venous angle. It is thin-walled, often collapsed and beaded by its valves. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the azygos vein and the descending aorta; gently separate the tissue between them behind the esophagus to find the thin whitish duct.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Hemiazygos vein</b>: drains into azygos vein, crossing to the right behind the aorta at about T9; on top of left side of the lower thoracic vertebral bodies; under descending thoracic aorta in front</li><li><b>Accessory hemiazygos vein</b>: drains into azygos vein at about T7–T8, crossing behind the aorta (or joins the hemiazygos); on top of left side of the T5–T8 vertebral bodies; under descending thoracic aorta in front</li><li><b>Thoracic duct</b>: drains into left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins); on top of thoracic vertebral bodies, right posterior intercostal arteries and the hemiazygos crossings; under esophagus in front</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Hemiazygos vein","Accessory hemiazygos vein","Thoracic duct"]},{"l":"CAD-T3","h":"Sympathetic trunk and splanchnic nerves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Sympathetic trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Gray rami to every spinal nerve; cardiac, pulmonary and splanchnic branches to the viscera</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Heads of the ribs in the thorax; sides of the lumbar vertebral bodies in the abdomen</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal pleura (thorax); IVC (inferior vena cava) over the right lumbar trunk, aorta beside the left</dd></dl><p>The sympathetic trunk is a paired vertical chain of ganglia linked by nerve segments, lying behind the costal pleura on the heads of the upper ribs (and lower down on the sides of the vertebral bodies), joined to each intercostal nerve by rami communicantes; in the abdomen it passes posterior to the medial arcuate ligament and descends on the anterolateral lumbar vertebral bodies along the medial border of psoas major. <i>Finding it:</i> Strip the costal pleura from the posterior thoracic wall beside the vertebral bodies and look for the cord running vertically over the rib heads.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Celiac ganglion (foregut), after piercing the crus; some fibers reach the suprarenal medulla</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</dd></dl><p>The greater splanchnic nerve is formed by roots from the sympathetic ganglia of about T5-T9 (or T10) that leave the trunk medially and unite into a nerve descending obliquely over the sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies; it pierces the crus of the diaphragm and ends in the celiac ganglion, carrying preganglionic sympathetic fibres. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the sympathetic trunk on the rib heads, then follow the medial branches from its middle ganglia as they join into one nerve heading down onto the vertebral bodies.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_276_1652.jpg\" alt=\"Innervation of small intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.58%;top:5.20%;width:20.43%;height:4.12%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Sympathetic trunk: Innervation of small intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.51</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_423_467.jpg\" alt=\"Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:66.61%;top:66.78%;width:20.38%;height:3.75%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve: Nerve fibers passing through the abdominal prevertebral plexus and ganglia <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.158</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Sympathetic trunk</b>: comes from preganglionic fibers from spinal cord segments T1–L2, via white rami communicantes; on top of heads of the ribs in the thorax; under costal pleura (thorax)</li><li><b>Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve</b>: comes from sympathetic ganglia T5–T9 (preganglionic fibers leaving the trunk medially); on top of sides of the thoracic vertebral bodies, medial to the sympathetic trunk; under costal pleura and endothoracic fascia</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Sympathetic trunk","Greater (thoracic) splanchnic nerve"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Ventricles and interventricular septum","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Posterior interventricular sulcus</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior part of the interventricular septum, deep to the groove</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein lie in it; diaphragm beneath</dd></dl><p>The groove on the diaphragmatic (inferior) surface between the right and left ventricles, running from the crux, where it meets the coronary sulcus, toward the apex. It contains the posterior interventricular artery and the middle cardiac vein in fat. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the heart and look at the surface that rested on the diaphragm; follow the coronary sinus to the crux, and the groove running from there toward the apex is the posterior interventricular sulcus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_214_186.jpg\" alt=\"Diaphragmatic surface of the heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:50.19%;top:94.93%;width:26.52%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Posterior interventricular sulcus: Diaphragmatic surface of the heart <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.59</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Posterior interventricular sulcus</b>: on top of posterior part of the interventricular septum, deep to the groove; under posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein lie in it</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Posterior interventricular sulcus"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Great vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior vena cava</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Right and left brachiocephalic veins, joining behind the right 1st costal cartilage; receives the azygos</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right pulmonary artery and right superior pulmonary vein behind; right vagus and trachea higher up</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right phrenic nerve and pleura on its right; thymus and right lung edge in front</dd></dl><p>Formed behind the right first costal cartilage by the two brachiocephalic veins, it descends vertically on the right side of the ascending aorta, receives the arch of the azygos vein on its posterior side at about T4, and enters the upper right atrium at the level of the third costal cartilage; its lower half is inside the pericardium. The right phrenic nerve runs down its right lateral surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ascending aorta inside the pericardium; the thin-walled vein on its right side running down into the right atrium is the SVC.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pulmonary trunk</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left pulmonary arteries, dividing under the aortic arch at sternal-angle level</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Ascending aorta to its right; transverse pericardial sinus and left atrium behind</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage; left auricle curls around its left side</dd></dl><p>The thick vessel leaving the conus arteriosus of the right ventricle, starting anterior and to the left of the ascending aorta and passing posteriorly along its left side. It divides in the concavity of the aortic arch into the right and left pulmonary arteries, with the ligamentum arteriosum attached near the fork. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the most anterior great vessel leaving the front of the heart, to the left of the ascending aorta; follow it up to where it forks under the arch.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_195_1508.jpg\" alt=\"Lateral exposures of mediastinum\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:79.35%;top:50.31%;width:15.36%;height:3.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior vena cava: Lateral exposures of mediastinum <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.70</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_169_1459.jpg\" alt=\"Interior of right ventricle of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.80%;top:8.70%;width:14.69%;height:4.88%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pulmonary trunk: Interior of right ventricle of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.54</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ascending aorta</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left ventricle, through the aortic valve</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right and left coronary arteries (its only branches); continues as the arch at the sternal angle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root; then pericardium and sternum</dd></dl><p>The first part of the aorta, about 5 cm long and inside the pericardium, running superiorly and to the right from the aortic valve to become the arch at the level of the sternal angle. Its branches are the right and left coronary arteries, which arise from the aortic sinuses just above the valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk leaving the front of the heart; the larger vessel just to its right, between it and the superior vena cava, is the ascending aorta.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior vena cava</b>: drains into right atrium, upper part, at the level of the right 3rd costal cartilage; on top of right pulmonary artery and right superior pulmonary vein behind; under right phrenic nerve and pleura on its right</li><li><b>Pulmonary trunk</b>: comes from right ventricle, via the conus arteriosus and pulmonary valve; on top of ascending aorta to its right; under pericardium, then the left 2nd costal cartilage</li><li><b>Ascending aorta</b>: comes from left ventricle, through the aortic valve; on top of transverse pericardial sinus, then the right pulmonary artery and left atrium behind it; under right auricle and pulmonary trunk overlap its root</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Superior vena cava","Pulmonary trunk","Ascending aorta"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Left coronary artery and branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Anterior two-thirds of septum, front of both ventricles and apex, via diagonal and septal branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; great cardiac vein runs beside it</dd></dl><p>A terminal branch of the left coronary artery that runs in the anterior interventricular sulcus from the left side of the pulmonary trunk down to the apex, often turning around it to meet the posterior interventricular artery. It gives diagonal branches onto the left ventricle and septal branches into the anterior part of the interventricular septum, and it runs with the great cardiac vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle; trace the left coronary artery from between them to the branch that turns straight down the front of the heart.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_176_1474.jpg\" alt=\"Variations in distribution of coronary arteries\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.01%;top:72.10%;width:34.87%;height:16.46%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD): Variations in distribution of coronary arteries <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.60</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)</b>: comes from left coronary artery, one of its two terminal branches; on top of anterior interventricular sulcus, over the anterior interventricular septum; under epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Anterior interventricular artery (LAD)"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Cardiac veins and coronary sinus","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Middle cardiac vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Diaphragmatic surfaces of both ventricles, from near the apex</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Coronary sinus, near its right end</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Epicardial fat and visceral pericardium; diaphragm below the heart</dd></dl><p>Begins near the apex and ascends in the posterior interventricular sulcus beside the posterior interventricular artery, ending in the coronary sinus near its right end. It is the vein of the groove between the two ventricles on the diaphragmatic surface. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the coronary sinus on the back of the heart; the vein entering it from the groove that runs down toward the apex is the middle cardiac vein.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_177_1477.jpg\" alt=\"Cardiac veins\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:88.45%;top:80.80%;width:10.29%;height:10.34%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Middle cardiac vein: Cardiac veins <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.61</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Middle cardiac vein</b>: drains into coronary sinus, near its right end; on top of posterior interventricular sulcus, beside the posterior interventricular artery; under epicardial fat and visceral pericardium</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Middle cardiac vein"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Inside the right atrium","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Crista terminalis</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Lateral right atrial wall, under the sulcus terminalis; SA (sinoatrial) node at its top</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium only, facing the right atrial cavity</dd></dl><p>A smooth muscular ridge running down the lateral inner wall of the right atrium from the opening of the superior vena cava to the opening of the inferior vena cava. It separates the rough pectinate anterior wall from the smooth sinus venarum, is marked outside by the sulcus terminalis, and has the sinoatrial node near its upper end. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the two vena caval openings; the ridge running between them along the lateral wall, where rough wall meets smooth wall, is the crista terminalis.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pectinate muscles</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</dd></dl><p>Parallel muscular ridges on the inner surface of the anterior wall of the right atrium and inside both auricles, running forward from the crista terminalis. In the left atrium they are confined to the auricle, and the rest of that chamber is smooth. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right atrium and find the crista terminalis; the ridges running forward from it across the anterior wall and into the auricle are the pectinate muscles.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_168_1458.jpg\" alt=\"Right atrium of heart\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.57%;top:59.43%;width:14.13%;height:4.75%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Crista terminalis: Right atrium of heart <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.53</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_442_1973.jpg\" alt=\"Transitions occurring at pectinate line\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.92%;top:60.40%;width:95.30%;height:5.84%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pectinate muscles: Transitions occurring at pectinate line <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.59</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Crista terminalis</b>: on top of lateral right atrial wall, under the sulcus terminalis; under endocardium only, facing the right atrial cavity</li><li><b>Pectinate muscles</b>: on top of anterior right atrial wall and auricle, running forward from the crista terminalis; under endocardium, facing the right atrial cavity</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"5","ks":["Crista terminalis","Pectinate muscles"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Inside the ventricles","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Trabeculae carneae</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</dd></dl><p>Irregular muscular ridges, bridges and columns on the inner walls of both ventricles, coarse in the right ventricle and finer in the left. The outflow parts (conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule) are smooth. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle; the rough, interlacing muscle on the walls below the valves, apart from the large papillary muscles, is the trabeculae carneae.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Base of the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium; the right bundle branch runs inside it</dd></dl><p>A curved muscular band in the right ventricle running from the lower part of the interventricular septum across the cavity to the base of the anterior papillary muscle. It carries the right bundle branch of the conducting system; a comparable band is usually absent in the left ventricle. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the right ventricle, find the large anterior papillary muscle, and follow the band running from its base back to the septum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_220_192.jpg\" alt=\"Internal view of the right ventricle\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:44.11%;top:94.98%;width:18.09%;height:4.00%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Trabeculae carneae: Internal view of the right ventricle <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.64</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_223_199.jpg\" alt=\"Internal view of the left ventricle\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.62%;top:57.85%;width:16.37%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Papillary muscle: Internal view of the left ventricle <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 3.67</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Papillary muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; left: anterior, posterior)</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Chordae tendineae, to the cusps of the tricuspid or mitral valve</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Endocardium; chordae tendineae attach at its tip</dd></dl><p>Conical muscular projections from the ventricular wall whose tips give attachment to the chordae tendineae of the atrioventricular cusps. The right ventricle usually has anterior (largest), posterior and septal papillary muscles; the left ventricle has two large ones, anterior and posterior. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle and follow the chordae tendineae down from a valve cusp; the muscle mound they attach to is a papillary muscle.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Chordae tendineae</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Fibrous collagen cords covered by endocardium</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Papillary muscle tips to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of tricuspid and mitral cusps</dd></dl><p>Thin fibrous cords running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the free edges and ventricular surfaces of the tricuspid and mitral cusps, present in both ventricles. The semilunar (pulmonary and aortic) valves have no chordae. <i>Finding it:</i> Open a ventricle, find a cusp of the atrioventricular valve, and follow the threads hanging from its edge down to a papillary muscle.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Trabeculae carneae</b>: on top of inner walls of both ventricles (absent from the smooth outflow tracts); under endocardium, facing the ventricular cavity</li><li><b>Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)</b>: arises from lower interventricular septum, on the right ventricular side; on top of free across the right ventricular cavity, bridging septum to anterior wall; under endocardium</li><li><b>Papillary muscle</b>: arises from inner ventricular wall (right: anterior, posterior, septal; on top of ventricular wall muscle, from which it projects into the cavity; under endocardium</li><li><b>Chordae tendineae</b>: The chordae tendineae are thin cords in both ventricles running from the tips of the papillary muscles to the edges of the tricuspid and mitral valve cusps.</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"6","ks":["Trabeculae carneae","Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)","Papillary muscle","Chordae tendineae"]},{"l":"CAD-T4","h":"Heart valves","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Aortic valve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left atrium behind it; anterior mitral cusp continuous with its base</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front; left sternum at the 3rd intercostal space</dd></dl><p>The semilunar valve between the left ventricle and the ascending aorta, with right, left and posterior cusps and no chordae; the right and left coronary arteries open from the aortic sinuses above the right and left cusps. It lies near the center of the heart, posterior to the left half of the sternum at about the 3rd intercostal space, posterior and to the right of the pulmonary valve. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the ascending aorta just above the heart and look down; the three cusps at its base, with coronary openings above two of them, form the aortic valve.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_170_1462.jpg\" alt=\"Valves of heart and great vessels\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:79.70%;top:60.72%;width:10.35%;height:3.83%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Aortic valve: Valves of heart and great vessels <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 1.55</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Aortic valve</b>: on top of left atrium behind it; under pulmonary valve and right ventricular outflow in front</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"7","ks":["Aortic valve"]},{"l":"CAD-A1","h":"Flat abdominal muscles, outside in","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Internal oblique muscle</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Arises from</dt><dd>Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Inserts into</dt><dd>Inferior borders of ribs 10–12, linea alba, and pubic crest via the conjoint tendon</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>External oblique muscle</dd></dl><p>Middle of the three flat muscles, directly deep to external oblique: its fibres fan out from the thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of the iliac crest and lateral part of the inguinal ligament, running mostly superomedially, and its aponeurosis splits around rectus abdominis above the arcuate line. <i>Finding it:</i> Cut and reflect external oblique; the next fleshy layer, with fibres crossing those of external oblique, is internal oblique; its lowest fibres arch over the spermatic cord and continue onto it as cremaster.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_218_1560.jpg\" alt=\"Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:40.49%;top:5.69%;width:17.85%;height:4.87%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Internal oblique muscle: Structure of anterolateral abdominal wall <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.6</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Internal oblique muscle</b>: arises from thoracolumbar fascia, anterior two-thirds of iliac crest, lateral two-thirds of inguinal ligament; on top of transversus abdominis, with the thoracoabdominal nerves in the plane between them; under external oblique muscle</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Internal oblique muscle"]},{"l":"CAD-A1","h":"Epigastric arteries and inguinal region","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior epigastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>External iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lower rectus abdominis; anastomoses with the superior epigastric artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath</dd></dl><p>Branch of the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament that ascends medial to the deep inguinal ring beneath the peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line and runs on the deep surface of rectus abdominis, usually with two companion veins. <i>Finding it:</i> From inside the lower abdominal wall, find the deep inguinal ring with the ductus deferens or round ligament entering it; the vessel climbing along its medial edge toward rectus is the inferior epigastric artery.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ilioinguinal nerve</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Skin of medial thigh, penile root or mons, anterior scrotum or labium majus; lower internal oblique</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; then the spermatic cord (or round ligament) in the inguinal canal</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Kidney at first; in the inguinal canal, the external oblique aponeurosis</dd></dl><p>Branch of L1 that emerges from the lateral border of psoas, crosses quadratus lumborum and iliacus just below the iliohypogastric nerve, and pierces transversus abdominis and internal oblique near the anterior iliac crest. It then runs through the inguinal canal alongside (outside) the spermatic cord or round ligament and exits through the superficial inguinal ring. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the external oblique aponeurosis over the inguinal canal; the thin white nerve lying on the front of the spermatic cord and emerging with it at the superficial ring is the ilioinguinal.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_376_1869.jpg\" alt=\"Pelvic fascial ligaments\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:70.76%;top:16.07%;width:13.49%;height:4.66%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior epigastric artery: Pelvic fascial ligaments <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.14</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_426_472.jpg\" alt=\"Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.72%;top:32.36%;width:14.81%;height:3.80%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ilioinguinal nerve: Lumbar plexus in the posterior abdominal region <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.161</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Inferior epigastric artery</b>: comes from external iliac artery, just above the inguinal ligament; on top of parietal peritoneum (raising the lateral umbilical fold), then posterior rectus sheath above the arcuate line; under transversalis fascia, then rectus abdominis once inside the rectus sheath</li><li><b>Ilioinguinal nerve</b>: comes from L1 anterior ramus (with the iliohypogastric nerve); on top of quadratus lumborum behind the kidney; under kidney at first</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Inferior epigastric artery","Ilioinguinal nerve"]},{"l":"CAD-A2","h":"Duodenum, pancreas and spleen","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right psoas major, IVC (inferior vena cava), aorta and right ureter, at L3</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Superior mesenteric artery and vein, and the root of the mesentery</dd></dl><p>The inferior (horizontal, third) part of the duodenum crosses from right to left at L3, posterior to the superior mesenteric artery and vein and the root of the mesentery, and anterior to the IVC, aorta, right psoas and right ureter, just below the uncinate process. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMA and SMV descending into the mesentery; the transverse loop of bowel passing behind them, in front of the aorta, is the third part.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Spleen</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally; left kidney medially</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach anteriorly (gastric impression); left colic flexure below</dd></dl><p>The spleen is a solid oval organ in the left hypochondrium lying against the diaphragm opposite ribs 9-11, posterolateral to the stomach, lateral to the left kidney and above the left colic flexure; its concave visceral surface has the hilum where the splenic vessels enter, and its superior border is usually notched. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the fundus of the stomach and follow the gastrosplenic ligament laterally to the solid organ tucked under the left dome of the diaphragm.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_328_1762.jpg\" alt=\"\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:4.75%;top:61.31%;width:28.51%;height:12.06%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum:  <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_272_1644.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of duodenum, pancreas, and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:90.69%;top:22.37%;width:8.46%;height:4.20%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Spleen: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of duodenum, pancreas, and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.46</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum</b>: on top of right psoas major, IVC (inferior vena cava), aorta and right ureter, at L3; under superior mesenteric artery and vein, and the root of the mesentery</li><li><b>Spleen</b>: on top of diaphragm against ribs 9–11 posterolaterally; under stomach anteriorly (gastric impression)</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Inferior (horizontal, 3rd) part of duodenum","Spleen"]},{"l":"CAD-A2","h":"Celiac trunk and left gastric artery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gastric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its smallest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Lesser curvature of the stomach and the abdominal esophagus; joins the right gastric artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature</dd></dl><p>The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk; it ascends retroperitoneally toward the esophagus, gives esophageal branches, then turns down along the lesser curvature within the lesser omentum to anastomose with the right gastric artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then take the branch that heads up to the esophagogastric junction before curving down the lesser curvature.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_260_1619.jpg\" alt=\"Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:8.36%;top:94.64%;width:14.67%;height:4.10%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left gastric artery: Nerves and lymphatics of abdominal esophagus and stomach <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.35</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Left gastric artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk (its smallest branch); on top of left crus of the diaphragm and the left suprarenal area, as it rises in the posterior wall; under peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the lesser omentum along the lesser curvature</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Left gastric artery"]},{"l":"CAD-A2","h":"Splenic artery and its branches","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Splenic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk (its largest branch)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Spleen; also pancreatic, short gastric, left gastro-omental and posterior gastric branches</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</dd></dl><p>The splenic artery is the largest branch of the celiac trunk; it follows a tortuous course to the left along the superior border of the pancreas, posterior to the stomach and lesser sac and anterior to the left kidney, then passes in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, then follow its left branch along the top of the pancreas toward the spleen.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Splenic artery, near the splenic hilum</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; anastomoses with the right gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; lesser sac behind</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd></dl><p>The left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery arises from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum, passes through the gastrosplenic ligament to the greater curvature, and runs to the right within the greater omentum, about a finger-breadth from the curvature, to anastomose with the right gastro-omental artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the splenic artery at the hilum of the spleen, then follow the branch that turns onto the greater curvature heading toward the pylorus.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_296_1694.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:39.44%;top:27.04%;width:13.00%;height:6.08%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Splenic artery: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of pancreas and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.61</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_264_1628.jpg\" alt=\"Omental bursa and stomach bed\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:82.92%;top:85.93%;width:16.58%;height:3.22%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery: Omental bursa and stomach bed <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.39</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Splenic artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk (its largest branch); on top of left suprarenal gland and left kidney, along the upper border of the pancreas; under stomach, separated by the lesser sac (omental bursa)</li><li><b>Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b>: comes from splenic artery, near the splenic hilum; on top of greater omentum, a finger-breadth below the greater curvature; under peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Splenic artery","Left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"]},{"l":"CAD-A2","h":"Common hepatic and gastroduodenal arteries","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Common hepatic artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Divides into gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries above the first part of duodenum</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</dd></dl><p>The common hepatic artery is the branch of the celiac trunk that runs to the right, retroperitoneally along the upper border of the pancreas, toward the superior part of the duodenum, where it divides into the gastroduodenal artery (down, behind the duodenum) and the proper hepatic artery (up, into the hepatoduodenal ligament). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the celiac trunk, take its right-going branch, and stop at the point where it splits near the first part of the duodenum.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gastroduodenal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Common hepatic artery</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; gives the posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>First part of the duodenum; a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it</dd></dl><p>The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and descends posterior to the superior (first) part of the duodenum, in front of the neck/head of the pancreas, with the bile duct usually to its right; at the lower border of the duodenum it divides into the right gastro-omental and anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the common hepatic artery, then follow the branch that dives down behind the first part of the duodenum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_374_409.jpg\" alt=\"Distribution of the common hepatic artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:68.95%;top:29.81%;width:23.03%;height:4.30%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Common hepatic artery: Distribution of the common hepatic artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.114</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_337_347.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal esophagus\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:76.79%;top:86.09%;width:22.35%;height:4.17%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Gastroduodenal artery: Abdominal esophagus <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.59</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Greater curvature and greater omentum; joins the left gastro-omental artery</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</dd></dl><p>The right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery that starts below the pylorus and runs to the left along the greater curvature between the layers of the greater omentum, supplying stomach and omentum and anastomosing with the left gastro-omental artery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the gastroduodenal artery emerging below the first part of the duodenum, then follow its branch to the left along the greater curvature.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Common hepatic artery</b>: comes from celiac trunk; on top of upper border of the pancreas, running right behind the lesser sac; under peritoneum of the lesser sac, then the pylorus and lesser omentum</li><li><b>Gastroduodenal artery</b>: comes from common hepatic artery; on top of neck and head of pancreas, beside the bile duct; under first part of the duodenum</li><li><b>Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery</b>: comes from gastroduodenal artery (terminal branch), below the pylorus; on top of head of pancreas at its start, then the greater omentum below the greater curvature; under peritoneum of the anterior layers of the greater omentum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Common hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery"]},{"l":"CAD-A3","h":"Liver and its lobes","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Right lobe of liver</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs</dd></dl><p>The largest anatomical lobe: everything to the right of the falciform ligament on the diaphragmatic surface and, on the visceral surface, to the right of the gallbladder fossa and the groove for the IVC. It extends lowest on the right and is related below to the right colic flexure, right kidney and duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament on the front of the liver, then the gallbladder and IVC on the underside; liver to the right of these is the right lobe.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left lobe of liver</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid</dd></dl><p>The smaller, thinner anatomical lobe, to the left of the falciform ligament on the diaphragmatic surface and to the left of the fissures for the ligamentum teres and ligamentum venosum on the visceral surface. It extends into the left upper abdomen anterior to the stomach and lesser omentum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament and the round ligament in its free edge, then follow them to the fissure on the underside; liver to the left is the left lobe.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_298_1699.jpg\" alt=\"Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:3.25%;top:8.25%;width:9.25%;height:7.15%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Right lobe of liver: Peritoneal and visceral relationships of liver <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.64</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Right lobe of liver</b>: on top of right kidney, right suprarenal gland, right colic flexure and duodenum; under right dome of the diaphragm and the lower right ribs</li><li><b>Left lobe of liver</b>: on top of stomach (anterior surface) and abdominal esophagus; under diaphragm and the anterior abdominal wall below the xiphoid</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Right lobe of liver","Left lobe of liver"]},{"l":"CAD-A3","h":"Peritoneal ligaments of the liver","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Falciform ligament</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery (ventral mesogastrium)</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, above the umbilicus</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Anterior and superior surface of the liver</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</dd></dl><p>Sickle-shaped, sagittal double fold of peritoneum attaching the anterior surface of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm above the umbilicus; its free inferior edge contains the round ligament of the liver, and it marks the surface division between the anatomical right and left lobes. <i>Finding it:</i> Open the upper abdomen in the midline; follow the fold from the umbilicus up onto the anterior surface of the liver: that fold is the falciform ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Obliterated left umbilical vein</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Umbilicus to the left branch of the portal vein, via the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it</dd></dl><p>The fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein, running from the umbilicus in the free inferior edge of the falciform ligament to the fissure for the ligamentum teres on the visceral surface, where it joins the left branch of the portal vein. On the underside it separates the left lobe from the quadrate lobe. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the falciform ligament between the anterior abdominal wall and the liver, then feel along its free lower edge for the firm cord running to the underside of the liver.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_256_1611.jpg\" alt=\"Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.57%;top:15.55%;width:14.58%;height:7.99%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Falciform ligament: Abdominal contents in situ and in relation to alimentary system <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.31</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_250_1604.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of greater and lesser omenta\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.47%;top:10.80%;width:13.44%;height:4.14%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis): Parts of greater and lesser omenta <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.26</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Coronary ligament (of liver)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Made from</dt><dd>Ventral mesentery; peritoneum reflecting from the diaphragm onto the liver</dd><dt>Connects</dt><dd>Diaphragm to the superior and posterior surface of the liver, enclosing the bare area</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Inferior surface of the diaphragm</dd></dl><p>The peritoneal reflection from the diaphragm onto the posterosuperior surface of the liver, with an anterior (superior) and a posterior (inferior) layer that enclose the bare area. The anterior layer is continuous with the falciform ligament, and the two layers meet at the right and left triangular ligaments. <i>Finding it:</i> Run a hand over the top of the right lobe toward the back until it is stopped by the peritoneal reflection to the diaphragm; that reflection is the coronary ligament.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Bare area of liver</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Diaphragm directly, with no peritoneum; IVC (inferior vena cava) and right suprarenal medially</dd></dl><p>The part of the posterior diaphragmatic surface of the right lobe not covered by peritoneum, in direct contact with the diaphragm and enclosed by the layers of the coronary ligament; the IVC lies in a groove at its medial side. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the IVC groove on the back of the liver, then the dull, non-shiny area to its right bounded by the coronary ligament layers.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Falciform ligament</b>: on top of anterior and superior surface of the liver; under parietal peritoneum of the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm</li><li><b>Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)</b>: on top of inferior surface of the liver, in the fissure for ligamentum teres; under free lower edge of the falciform ligament, which encloses it</li><li><b>Coronary ligament (of liver)</b>: on top of superior and posterior surface of the right lobe, around the bare area; under inferior surface of the diaphragm</li><li><b>Bare area of liver</b>: on top of diaphragm directly, with no peritoneum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Falciform ligament","Round ligament of liver (ligamentum teres hepatis)","Coronary ligament (of liver)","Bare area of liver"]},{"l":"CAD-A3","h":"Gallbladder and its parts","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gallbladder</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>First and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes)</dd></dl><p>A pear-shaped, thin-walled sac in the gallbladder fossa on the visceral surface of the liver, between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe, with its fundus projecting beyond the inferior border and its neck pointing toward the porta hepatis to continue as the cystic duct. Its body is related to the transverse colon and the superior part of the duodenum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior border of the right lobe at the right 9th costal cartilage in the midclavicular line, then lift the liver; the sac on its underside is the gallbladder.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_300_1709.jpg\" alt=\"Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:0.47%;top:54.74%;width:9.46%;height:4.55%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Gallbladder: Relationships of liver to other abdominal viscera, lesser omentum, and portal triad <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.66</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Gallbladder</b>: on top of first and second parts of the duodenum and the transverse colon; under liver (gallbladder fossa between right and quadrate lobes)</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Gallbladder"]},{"l":"CAD-A3","h":"Pancreas and spleen","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Pancreas</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left kidney, left suprarenal gland, aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava), behind the peritoneum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Stomach, separated by the lesser sac</dd></dl><p>The pancreas is a lobulated retroperitoneal gland lying across L1-L2 behind the stomach (with the lesser sac between), from the C of the duodenum on the right to the splenic hilum on the left; the splenic vein runs along its posterior surface and the splenic artery along its upper border. <i>Finding it:</i> Lift the stomach or open the lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament, then look at the posterior wall: the gland lying across the aorta is the pancreas.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_373_408.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply to the pancreas\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:75.15%;top:70.37%;width:9.15%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Pancreas: Arterial supply to the pancreas <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.113</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Pancreas</b>: on top of left kidney, left suprarenal gland, aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava), behind the peritoneum; under stomach, separated by the lesser sac</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Pancreas"]},{"l":"CAD-A4","h":"Small intestine: jejunum, ileum and mesentery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Jejunum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><p>The first two-fifths of the small intestine beyond the duodenum, starting at the duodenojejunal flexure and lying mostly in the upper left abdomen; it is wider, thicker-walled and redder than the ileum, with tall, closely packed circular folds inside. Its mesentery has little fat, usually 1-2 rows of large arcades and long vasa recta. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the duodenojejunal flexure left of L2 under the transverse mesocolon, then follow the loops away from it; hold the mesentery to the light to see the long vasa recta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ileum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><p>The distal three-fifths of the small intestine beyond the duodenum, lying mostly in the lower right abdomen and pelvis and ending at the ileocecal junction. Compared with the jejunum it is narrower, thinner-walled and paler, with low, sparse circular folds, Peyer patches, more mesenteric fat, many rows of small arcades and short vasa recta. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the cecum in the right iliac fossa, then follow backward the small intestine that enters its medial side; those terminal loops are ileum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_274_1650.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:82.87%;top:40.81%;width:9.03%;height:4.47%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Jejunum: Arterial supply and mesenteries of intestines <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.49</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_267_1632.jpg\" alt=\"Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:9.93%;top:79.28%;width:7.33%;height:4.19%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ileum: Lymphatic drainage and innervation of stomach and small intestine <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.42</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Jejunum</b>: on top of posterior abdominal wall over the left kidney, slung from the mesentery; under greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</li><li><b>Ileum</b>: on top of right posterior abdominal wall, iliac vessels and pelvic organs, slung from the mesentery; under greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Jejunum","Ileum"]},{"l":"CAD-A4","h":"Cecum, ileocecal junction and appendix","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Cecum</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</dd></dl><p>The first and widest part of the large intestine: a blind-ended sac in the right iliac fossa below the level of the ileocecal junction, with teniae coli and haustra and the appendix attached to its posteromedial side. It is usually covered by peritoneum but has no mesentery. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the terminal ileum and the ileocecal junction; the large sac hanging below that junction is the cecum, and its three teniae meet at the appendix.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_347_366.jpg\" alt=\"Cecum and appendix\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:2.79%;top:66.32%;width:12.76%;height:4.96%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Cecum: Cecum and appendix <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.77</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Cecum</b>: on top of right iliacus and psoas major, with the femoral and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves; under coils of ileum and the greater omentum, then the anterior abdominal wall</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Cecum"]},{"l":"CAD-A4","h":"Superior mesenteric artery to the small bowel","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior mesenteric artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Midgut: duodenum below the major papilla to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</dd></dl><p>A single large artery from the anterior aorta about 1 cm below the celiac trunk (L1) that runs down behind the neck of the pancreas, then in front of the left renal vein, uncinate process and third part of the duodenum into the root of the mesentery. Its jejunal and ileal branches leave its left side, the middle colic leaves its front, the right colic and ileocolic leave its right side, and the superior mesenteric vein lies on its right. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the neck of the pancreas and the third part of the duodenum, then the artery emerging between them just left of the superior mesenteric vein; trace it up to the aorta.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior mesenteric vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Neck of the pancreas at its end; the root of the mesentery below that</dd></dl><p>Drains the small intestine, cecum, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon, ascending in the root of the mesentery on the right side of the superior mesenteric artery, anterior to the third part of the duodenum and the uncinate process. Behind the neck of the pancreas it joins the splenic vein to form the hepatic portal vein. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the superior mesenteric artery below the pancreas, then take the wider, thin-walled vessel immediately to its right and follow it up behind the pancreatic neck.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_376_411.jpg\" alt=\"Superior mesenteric artery\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:52.50%;top:11.47%;width:34.21%;height:4.13%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior mesenteric artery: Superior mesenteric artery <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.116</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_266_1630.jpg\" alt=\"Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:11.90%;top:88.25%;width:22.04%;height:5.16%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior mesenteric vein: Veins of stomach, duodenum, and spleen <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.41</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior mesenteric artery</b>: comes from anterior abdominal aorta at L1, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk; on top of left renal vein, uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum; under neck of the pancreas and splenic vein, then the root of the mesentery</li><li><b>Superior mesenteric vein</b>: drains into joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the hepatic portal vein; on top of third part of the duodenum and uncinate process, right of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); under neck of the pancreas at its end</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"3","ks":["Superior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric vein"]},{"l":"CAD-A4","h":"Inferior mesenteric vessels and marginal artery","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Superior rectal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Upper rectum via two branches beside it; anastomoses with the middle rectal arteries</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front</dd></dl><p>The terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery beyond its sigmoid branches, descending in the root of the sigmoid mesocolon across the left common iliac vessels into the pelvis. At about S3 it divides into two branches that run on either side of the rectum. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the inferior mesenteric artery, then follow its main stem downward past the sigmoid branches over the pelvic brim to the back of the rectum.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_400_1903.jpg\" alt=\"Arteries and veins of rectum and anal canal\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.31%;top:1.84%;width:27.74%;height:5.40%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Superior rectal artery: Arteries and veins of rectum and anal canal <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 3.32</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Superior rectal artery</b>: comes from IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), its terminal continuation; on top of left common iliac vessels, then the sacrum (S3) behind the rectum; under peritoneum of the sigmoid mesocolon root, then the rectum in front</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"4","ks":["Superior rectal artery"]},{"l":"CAD-A5","h":"Inside the kidney, out to the ureter","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Ureter</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Formed by</dt><dd>Renal pelvis, at the ureteropelvic junction</dd><dt>Empties into</dt><dd>Posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels; mesentery root (right), sigmoid mesocolon (left)</dd></dl><p>A thick-walled muscular tube from the renal pelvis that descends retroperitoneally on the anterior surface of psoas major, adherent to the posterior parietal peritoneum and crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels. It enters the pelvis by crossing the bifurcation of the common iliac artery (or the start of the external iliac artery). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the renal pelvis at the hilum and follow the tube down on psoas to the pelvic brim; check that the gonadal vessels cross in front of it.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_319_1746.jpg\" alt=\"Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:80.90%;top:75.03%;width:6.37%;height:3.91%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Ureter: Posterior abdominal wall showing great vessels, kidneys, and suprarenal glands <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.76</small></figcaption></figure><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Ureter</b>: empties into posterolateral wall of the urinary bladder, opening at the trigone; on top of psoas major, then the common iliac bifurcation at the pelvic brim; under parietal peritoneum, crossed by gonadal and colic vessels</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"1","ks":["Ureter"]},{"l":"CAD-A5","h":"Renal and gonadal vessels","p":"<p class=\"sxn\"><b>Renal artery</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Kidney via five segmental arteries; also the inferior suprarenal artery and upper ureter</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Crus of the diaphragm and psoas major; renal pelvis behind it at the hilum</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Renal vein; the right also passes behind the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd></dl><p>Paired lateral branch of the aorta, usually at the L1/L2 disc just below the superior mesenteric artery; the right renal artery is longer and passes posterior to the IVC, and the left lies posterior to the left renal vein. At the hilum it usually lies between the renal vein (anterior) and renal pelvis (posterior), often dividing into segmental branches before entering, and accessory renal arteries are common. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the aorta just below the SMA origin and follow the lateral branch to the kidney; at the hilum check that the vein is in front of it and the pelvis behind it.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left renal vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Left kidney, plus the left suprarenal and left gonadal veins</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left renal artery, then the abdominal aorta, which it crosses in front of</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>SMA (superior mesenteric artery) at the midline; pancreas and splenic vein laterally</dd></dl><p>It leaves the left renal hilum anterior to the renal artery, crosses anterior to the aorta in the angle just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery, and ends in the left side of the IVC. It is longer than the right renal vein and receives the left suprarenal vein (upper border) and left gonadal vein (lower border). <i>Finding it:</i> Find the SMA origin on the front of the aorta; the vein running horizontally between the SMA (in front) and the aorta (behind) is the left renal vein. Trace it left to the kidney and right into the IVC.</p><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_moore_325_1756.jpg\" alt=\"Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:1.78%;top:54.02%;width:18.16%;height:8.16%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Renal artery: Arterial supply of kidneys and ureters <small>· Moore's Clinically Oriented Anatomy, Fig. 2.84</small></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"sf tbk\"><div class=\"pimg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"../img/tb/v3_gray_405_445.jpg\" alt=\"Renal vasculature\"><span class=\"ovb\" style=\"left:67.76%;top:89.73%;width:12.13%;height:4.57%\"></span></div><figcaption><span class=\"tbb\">Textbook</span>Left renal vein: Renal vasculature <small>· Gray's Anatomy for Students, Fig. 4.142</small></figcaption></figure><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Drains</dt><dd>Pampiniform plexus (testis) or ovarian venous plexus (ovary)</dd><dt>Drains into</dt><dd>Left renal vein, entering at a right angle</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Left psoas major and the left ureter, which it crosses in front of</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed in front by the left colic vessels and the descending colon</dd></dl><p>It forms from the pampiniform plexus (testis) or the ovarian plexus, ascends on the left psoas major beside the gonadal artery and anterior to the ureter, and ends at about a right angle in the lower border of the left renal vein. The right gonadal vein differs: it enters the IVC directly. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the left renal vein crossing the aorta, then follow the vein entering its lower border down along psoas.</p><p class=\"sxn\"><b>Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)</b></p><dl class=\"facts\"><dt>Comes from</dt><dd>Anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2)</dd><dt>Supplies</dt><dd>Testis via the spermatic cord, or ovary via the suspensory ligament of the ovary</dd><dt>On top of</dt><dd>Psoas major and the ureter; the right one also crosses in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</dd><dt>Underneath</dt><dd>Parietal peritoneum; crossed by the duodenum and colic vessels (right) or left colic vessels (left)</dd></dl><p>A slender paired branch from the front of the aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2) that runs inferolaterally on psoas major deep to the peritoneum with its gonadal vein, crossing anterior to the ureter (the right one also crosses in front of the IVC). The testicular artery enters the deep inguinal ring to run in the spermatic cord; the ovarian artery crosses the pelvic brim into the suspensory ligament of the ovary. <i>Finding it:</i> Find the ureter on psoas; the thin vessels crossing in front of it obliquely are the gonadal vessels. Follow the narrower one up to its origin on the aorta just below the renal arteries.</p><div class=\"rrv\"><h4>Rapid review</h4><ul><li><b>Renal artery</b>: comes from lateral abdominal aorta at L1–L2, just below the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); on top of crus of the diaphragm and psoas major; under renal vein</li><li><b>Left renal vein</b>: drains into left side of the IVC (inferior vena cava); on top of left renal artery, then the abdominal aorta, which it crosses in front of; under SMA (superior mesenteric artery) at the midline</li><li><b>Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein</b>: drains into left renal vein, entering at a right angle; on top of left psoas major and the left ureter, which it crosses in front of; under parietal peritoneum</li><li><b>Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)</b>: comes from anterior abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries (about L2); on top of psoas major and the ureter; under parietal peritoneum</li></ul></div>","s":"","sn":"","pn":"2","ks":["Renal artery","Left renal vein","Left gonadal (testicular / ovarian) vein","Gonadal artery (testicular / ovarian)"]}]};
